Rumen Georgiev Radev [LINK: ([a]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-1]  (born 18 June 1963) is a Bulgarian politician and former  [LINK: (Bulgarian Air Force) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_Air_Force]  officer who served as the  [LINK: (president of Bulgaria) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Bulgaria]  from 2017 until his resignation in 2026, the first to do so in Bulgaria's  [LINK: (post-communist history) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Bulgaria_(1990%E2%80%93present)] .

Born in  [LINK: (Dimitrovgrad) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dimitrovgrad,_Bulgaria] , Radev served as higher commander of the Bulgarian Air Force prior to his term in office, [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-CV-2]  holding the rank of  [LINK: (major general) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Major_general] . He won the  [LINK: (2016 presidential election) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2016_Bulgarian_presidential_election]  as an  [LINK: (independent) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Independent_politician]  candidate supported by the  [LINK: (Bulgarian Socialist Party) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_Socialist_Party] , defeating  [LINK: (GERB) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GERB]  candidate  [LINK: (Tsetska Tsacheva) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsetska_Tsacheva]  in the second round. [LINK: ([2]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-bnr.bg-3]  [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-4]  [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-5]  [LINK: ([5]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-6]  He won a second consecutive term in the  [LINK: (2021 election) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_Bulgarian_general_election] , with 66% of the vote in the second round. [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:0-7]  [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-8]


# EARLY LIFE #

Rumen Georgiev Radev was born on 18 June 1963 in  [LINK: (Dimitrovgrad, Bulgaria) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dimitrovgrad,_Bulgaria] . His family is from  [LINK: (Slavyanovo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slavyanovo,_Haskovo_Province]  in the  [LINK: (Haskovo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haskovo]  region. In 1982, he graduated from the Mathematical School in Haskovo with a gold medal.


# MILITARY CAREER #

Radev graduated from the Georgi Benkovski Bulgarian Air Force University in 1987 as the top graduate. In 1992, he graduated from the  [LINK: (US Air Force) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Air_Force]   [LINK: (Squadron Officer School) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Squadron_Officer_School]  at Maxwell AFB. From 1994 to 1996, he studied at the  [LINK: (Rakovski Defence and Staff College) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rakovski_Defence_and_Staff_College] , where he was also the top graduate. He holds a Doctor of Military Sciences degree in the field of improvement of tactical training of flight crews and simulation of air combat. [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-9]  In 2003, he graduated from  [LINK: (Air War College) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Air_War_College]  or  [LINK: (Air University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Air_University_(United_States_Air_Force)]  at  [LINK: (Maxwell AFB) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maxwell_Air_Force_Base]  in the United States with a Master of Strategic Studies with honors. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-CV-2]


# PRESIDENT OF BULGARIA (2016–26) #

In August 2016, the opposition  [LINK: (Bulgarian Socialist Party) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_Socialist_Party]  and the  [LINK: (Alternative for Bulgarian Revival) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alternative_for_Bulgarian_Revival]  (ABR) officially nominated Radev as a candidate for the  [LINK: (November 2016 presidential election) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2016_Bulgarian_presidential_election] . [LINK: ([2]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-bnr.bg-3]  [LINK: ([9]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-10]  [LINK: ([10]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-11]  In the same month, ABR withdrew its presidential nomination of General Radev [LINK: ([11]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-12]  in favour of  [LINK: (Ivaylo Kalfin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ivaylo_Kalfin] .

In the first round of the election, conducted on 6 November 2016, Radev came first with 25.44% of the vote. [LINK: ([12]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-13]  He faced  [LINK: (GERB) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GERB]  candidate  [LINK: (Tsetska Tsacheva) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsetska_Tsacheva]  in the runoff the following Sunday 13 November. He defeated her, winning 59.37% of the popular vote. [LINK: ([13]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-14]

On 1 February 2021, he officially announced that he and  [LINK: (Iliana Iotova) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova]  would run for a second term. [LINK: ([14]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-15]  The presidential election happened on 14 November 2021. Prior to the election, several parties declared their support for Radev, including  [LINK: (ITN) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/There_Is_Such_a_People] ,  [LINK: (PP) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/We_Continue_the_Change]  and  [LINK: (BSPzB) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BSP_for_Bulgaria] . Radev received 1,322,385 votes in the first round, alongside his running partner  [LINK: (Iliana Iotova) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova] , 49.42% of the vote. [LINK: ([15]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:3-16]  This led to a second round run-off with the  [LINK: (GERB) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GERB] -supported candidate  [LINK: (Anastas Gerdzhikov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anastas_Gerdzhikov] , who got 22.83% of the vote in the first round. [LINK: ([15]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:3-16]  [LINK: ([16]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-PoliticoResultsNov2021-17]  Radev won in the run-off, with 66.7% of the vote, starting his second term as president. [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:0-7]

Since his election into office, Radev has frequently criticised Bulgarian prime minister  [LINK: (Boyko Borisov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boyko_Borisov] , whom Radev views as permitting  [LINK: (corruption) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corruption_in_Bulgaria]  through a 'reckless leadership style', as well as attempting to strangle his political opposition. This led Radev to frequently  [LINK: (veto) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Veto]  legislative proposals submitted by Borisov's  [LINK: (GERB) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GERB]  party to  [LINK: (Bulgarian Parliament) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_Parliament] , issuing a total of 19 vetoes in his first two and a half years of his presidency. [LINK: ([17]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-18]  [LINK: ([18]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-19]  [LINK: ([19]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-20]  [LINK: ([20]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-21]  Borisov, on the other hand, often accused Radev of 'sabotaging the government's work', as well as supporting the opposition  [LINK: (Socialist Party) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_Socialist_Party]  during campaign periods. [LINK: ([21]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-22]  [LINK: ([22]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-23]

In his 2019  [LINK: (New Year) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Year] 's address to the Bulgarian People, broadcast on almost all Bulgarian TV channels, Radev stated that he believed that  [LINK: (Borisov's Government) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Third_Borisov_Government]  had failed in addressing  [LINK: (corruption) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corruption_in_Bulgaria] , placed the country in  [LINK: (economic stagnation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Economic_stagnation]  with price increases and low wages, undermined the fairness of  [LINK: (elections) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elections_in_Bulgaria] , as well as 'retreated' from law and justice. [LINK: ([23]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-24]  [LINK: ([24]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-25]

In June 2019, Radev vetoed a major government contract for the acquisition of 16  [LINK: (F-16 Fighting Falcons) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_Dynamics_F-16_Fighting_Falcon]  Block 70 from the  [LINK: (United States) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States]  at a cost of around 2bln  [LINK: (lev) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_lev] . [LINK: ([25]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-26]  Radev strongly criticised the deal, accusing the government of  [LINK: (authoritarianism) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Authoritarianism]  and stated that he believed it had agreed to downgrades in the jets'  [LINK: (avionics) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avionics]  and armament, in order to get a lower purchasing price, which he also deemed too high for what they are worth. He added, that as a former  [LINK: (pilot) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aircraft_pilot]  and air force commander, he did not believe that the deal was in Bulgaria's best interests. [LINK: ([26]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-27]  [LINK: ([27]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-28]  The pro-government majority in Bulgaria's national assembly overruled Radev's veto and the deal was nonetheless concluded. [LINK: ([28]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-29]  [LINK: ([29]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-30]

In November 2019, Radev refused to sign the decree appointing  [LINK: (Ivan Geshev) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ivan_Geshev]  to the post of Chief Public Prosecutor of Bulgaria, following the latter's election to the post by Bulgaria's Supreme Judicial Council. He did not officially declare the motive for his refusal in written form, instead deciding to explain it personally to the media. Radev remarked that Geshev was the only candidate for the post and opined that the single-candidate nature of his election was supported by Borisov's government. He expressed the opinion that Geshev's candidacy had been supported only by government-controlled institutions and not by civic ones, which in his eyes led to a lack of public confidence in the institution. [LINK: ([30]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-31]  [LINK: ([31]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-32]  [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-33]  The Supreme Judicial Council refused to revise their decision and voted in favour of Geshev a second time, which triggered a constitutional requirement for Radev as president to sign the decree. [LINK: ([33]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-34]  Stating that he would refuse to violate the constitution, Radev did so following a meeting with Geshev, but called for changes to Bulgaria's constitution. [LINK: ([34]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-35]

The relations between Radev and the newly appointed general prosecutor quickly soured, as Geshev released what he stated was a  [LINK: (wiretap) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Telephone_tapping]  of Radev discussing his involvement in alleged criminal activities. Geshev further appealed to the  [LINK: (Constitutional Court of Bulgaria) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constitutional_Court_of_Bulgaria]  to have Radev's  [LINK: (legal immunity) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legal_immunity]  revoked. The general prosecutor's actions backfired in the eyes of the Bulgarian people and were widely viewed as an attempt to suppress and censor the president – either as 'revenge' for the president's initial veto of Geshev's appointment, or as a preparation for a move to remove Radev from his post as president. [LINK: ([35]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-36]  [LINK: ([36]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-37]  In response, Radev accused Geshev's prosecution of being controlled by Borisov's government, whom he accused of using both the prosecution, the secret services and the  [LINK: (National Police Service) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Police_Service_(Bulgaria)]  to crush dissent. [LINK: ([37]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-38]

Borisov swore that he had not ordered Radev to be wiretapped, [LINK: ([38]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-39]  but Radev doubled down – noting that the agency responsible for wiretapping in Bulgaria, the  [LINK: (State Agency for National Security) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/State_Agency_for_National_Security] , was directly responsible to the government and the prime minister. He further questioned the motives as to why it appeared to him as though the general prosecutor "saw crime and corruption in everything, except for the council of ministers". [LINK: ([39]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-40]

On 4 February 2020, Radev announced that he had formally withdrawn  [LINK: (confidence) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Confidence_and_supply]  in Borisov's government. He pointed out that there was, in his opinion, a strong crisis in the governance of all levels, a lack of will to reform and fight corruption, and a state of morally-questionable lawlessness in the country. Borisov accused Radev of trying to "take over" the country and stated that his government did not depend on Radev for confidence, adding that he believed the presidency to be a useless post, holding only 'symbolic councils', which he asserted never decided anything. [LINK: ([40]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-41]  [LINK: ([41]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-42]

After the start of the  [LINK: (COVID-19 pandemic in Bulgaria) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19_pandemic_in_Bulgaria] , Radev and Borisov's government initially appeared to thaw their relations for the sake of national unity during a time of crisis. [LINK: ([42]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-43]  This détente, however, was not to last – the two entered into another conflict shortly thereafter, with Radev partially vetoing a law passed by the Bulgarian government imposing additional measures in relation to the coronavirus pandemic. Radev objected to a paragraph criminalizing the spread of "fake news" with a fine of up to 5,000 euros. Surprisingly, Radev also vetoed a paragraph added on the insistence of the  [LINK: (Bulgarian Socialist Party) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_Socialist_Party] , which was supposed to impose  [LINK: (price controls) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Price_controls]  on essential goods. The veto sparked anger in Borisov, who accused Radev of populism and political opportunism. Despite this, Borisov ordered his parliamentary group to accept the veto on the two paragraphs – removing both the fake news fine and the socialist-added price control paragraphs from the final version of the law. The amended bill, however, still featured a paragraph which obligated telecom providers to track and store their user's data for up to 6 months and provide it upon request of the authorities, with the stated goal of tracking the movements of quarantined citizens. [LINK: ([43]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-44]

The two continued to clash over the coming days, with Radev frequently criticising the government for its handling of the state of emergency and accusing it of  [LINK: (quote mining) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quoting_out_of_context]  the  [LINK: (World Health Organization) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Health_Organization]  for political gains. In reply, Borisov accused Radev of sabotaging the state of emergency and compared Radev to a "dirty old hag of a mother-in-law, the nasty kind", expressing bewilderment at "how Radev was able to make [political] inflammatory statements on the day, in which his own [Radev's] father had passed away". Radev concluded, however, that the conflict was "only in Borisov's head", stating that he had never called for the state of the emergency to be lifted and merely disagreed with the government's handling of it. [LINK: ([44]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-45]  [LINK: ([45]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-46]

In October 2020, Radev attended an investment forum in  [LINK: (Estonia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Estonia] , but his visit was cut short after it was revealed that he had been exposed to a COVID-19 positive individual while in Bulgaria. [LINK: ([46]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-47]  Some sources alleged that Radev had been aware of that prior to travelling, while the president accused political opponents of deliberately orchestrating a campaign against him in order to tarnish his image, displaying a negative PCR test he had obtained prior to his official trip. [LINK: ([47]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-48]

In June 2020, photographs emerged that purported to show what appeared to be Prime Minister Borisov lying half-naked on a bed, next to a nightstand featuring a handgun and stacks of  [LINK: (euro) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euro]  banknotes. Borisov confirmed that the room in which the photos were taken was his, but denied the gun and money, stating that the images could have been manipulated. Borisov accused President Radev of flying a  [LINK: (consumer drone) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unmanned_aerial_vehicle]  into his residence in order to take the pictures. He also accused former Ombudswoman  [LINK: (Maya Manolova) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maya_Manolova] , TV star  [LINK: (Slavi Trifonov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slavi_Trifonov]  and his own former second in command  [LINK: (Tsvetan Tsvetanov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsvetan_Tsvetanov]  (who had just left and condemned the ruling party) of involvement in a plot to take photos of him while he was sleeping in a "KGB-Style"  [LINK: (kompromat) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kompromat]  operation. Radev condemned the leaks and called it an "insane" invasion of the prime minister's privacy. He added that he owns a drone, but that the accusation that he personally piloted it into the prime minister's residence to take pictures was part of Borisov's "fantasy and paranoia". [LINK: ([48]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-49]  [LINK: ([49]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-50]  [LINK: ([50]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-51]  [LINK: ([51]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-52]

In July 2020, agents of Chief Prosecutor Geshev entered the presidency and detained several of the president's advisors. This, alongside the photo scandal and an incident on a Burgas beach significantly impacted the credibility of Borisov's government, leading to the beginning of  [LINK: (large-scale anti-government protests) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020%E2%80%932021_Bulgarian_protests] , which Radev openly supported. Radev made a televised address to the nation, in which he demanded that both the entire government and the chief prosecutor resign, openly calling them " [LINK: (mafia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mafia] ". [LINK: ([52]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-53]

On 12 January 2021, Radev signed the election law, scheduling the next regular Bulgarian elections for 4 April. [LINK: ([53]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-54]  The elections came after a period of  [LINK: (public protest) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021-2023_Bulgarian_political_crisis]  against the  [LINK: (Borisov government) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Third_Borisov_Government] , and during a period of health restrictions caused by the  [LINK: (COVID-19 pandemic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19_pandemic_in_Bulgaria] . Radev voted using the newly installed  [LINK: (machine voting) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voting_machine]  system, and said that he voted for "a free, just and prosperous Bulgaria". [LINK: ([54]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-55]  After the elections, Radev decided to not postpone the convocation of the  [LINK: (Bulgarian National Assembly) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_National_Assembly] , [LINK: ([55]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-56]  despite the fragmented nature of the newly elected parliament. He also dismissed the possibility of convening a  [LINK: (Grand National Assembly) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_National_Assembly_of_Bulgaria] , as proposed by  [LINK: (GERB) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GERB]  leader Boyko Borisov. [LINK: ([56]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-57]

In his address to the opening session of the National Assembly, Radev expressed hope for a regular cabinet, highlighting the many issues facing Bulgaria: including poverty, the COVID-pandemic and corruption, however he also called on parties to avoid forming "unprincipled" coalitions. [LINK: ([57]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-58]  On 19 April, shortly after the opening session of parliament, Radev held consultations with all the parliamentary groups. [LINK: ([58]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-59]  During the consultations, Radev clashed with representatives from the GERB party, who accused him of expediting the procedure of consultations in order to impede government formations. [LINK: ([59]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-60]  He, in turn, denied that the Presidential institution would serve as an "architect" of future governments. [LINK: ([60]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-61]

After the failure of government formation, Radev announced the formation of a  [LINK: (caretaker cabinet) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Yanev_Government]  on 11 May, headed by his secretary of defense,  [LINK: (Stefan Yanev) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stefan_Yanev] , and scheduled snap legislative elections for 11 July. [LINK: ([61]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-62]  He announced that the caretaker government's priorities would be to organise fair elections without vote buying, sort out the supply of vaccines and present a Recovery and Development Plan to the European Union. [LINK: ([62]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-63]  Radev further hailed the cabinet as a government built on "consensus, combing experts from the left, center and right". On the same day, Radev officially dissolved the  [LINK: (45th National Assembly) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/45th_National_Assembly_of_Bulgaria] , noting that while it did not have a long term in office it would "be remembered for its role in dragging Bulgaria out of authoritarianism and corruption".

Radev generally praised the work of the First Yanev caretaker cabinet, especially highlighting the decrease in vote buying and invalid ballots, as well as attempts to reduce corruption within the state,  as a success. [LINK: ([63]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-64]  There were a number of cabinet changes in the  [LINK: (Second Yanev Government) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Yanev_Government] , with the Minister of Economy, Kiril Petkov, and Minister of Finance,  [LINK: (Assen Vassilev) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assen_Vassilev] , leaving to form the party "We Continue the Change". [LINK: ([64]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-65]  The appointment of the second Yanev caretaker cabinet made Radev the president who oversaw the most caretaker cabinets, as well as the only president to have appointed two consecutive caretaker cabinets.

The previous ruling party, GERB, consistently accused President Radev of using the caretaker cabinet as a "pre-election headquarters" and of trying to take over the state. [LINK: ([65]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-66]  During the second Yanev government's term, President Radev was accused by some individuals, notably those from the Bulgarian Socialist Party, of implicitly supporting the newly formed electoral coalition led by the former caretaker ministers. [LINK: ([66]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-67]  The tenure of the Yanev caretaker cabinet ended on 13 December, after the successful formation of a government led by Kiril Petkov. During the transfer of power to the new government, Radev praised the caretaker ministers, noting that they "stabilised the country" and "showed that a unity of purpose can be achieved across political lines". [LINK: ([67]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-68]

On 1 February 2021, Rumen Radev announced his intention to seek a second term as President of Bulgaria, with his running mate being the incumbent vice president,  [LINK: (Iliana Iotova) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova] . [LINK: ([68]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-69]  Radev declared that his candidacy was aimed at building a "stronger state" and continuing the message of change.

Radev's candidacy for president was endorsed by  [LINK: (ITN) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/There_is_Such_a_People] , [LINK: ([69]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-70]  the  [LINK: (BSP) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_Socialist_Party] , [LINK: ([70]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-71]   [LINK: (PP) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/We_Continue_the_Change]  [LINK: ([71]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-72]  and  [LINK: (ISBG) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stand_Up.BG] . [LINK: ([72]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-73]  This made Radev the favourite for the upcoming elections. [LINK: ([73]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-74]  Radev announced on 20 September that he had decided to run as an independent, meaning he was to be nominated by an "initiative committee". [LINK: ([74]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-75]  The initiative committee was composed of 189 individuals, including important political figures such as BSP MPs  [LINK: (Kristian Vigenin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kristian_Vigenin]  and  [LINK: (Vesela Lecheva) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vesela_Lecheva] , public activists  [LINK: (Nikolay Hadjigenov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nikolay_Hadjigenov]  and Arman Babikyan, as well as ITN MPs Stanislav Balabanov and Filip Stanev. [LINK: ([75]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-76]

Radev's re-election campaign faced a challenge after the nominations of the dean of  [LINK: (Sofia University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sofia_University] , Atanas Gerdjikov as the candidate of the GERB party, as well as the independent candidacy of the judge  [LINK: (Lozan Panov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lozan_Panov] , with the support of  [LINK: (Democratic Bulgaria) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democratic_Bulgaria] . [LINK: ([76]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-77]  On 17 October, Radev opened his campaign for president with a hike to the  [LINK: (Cherni Vrah) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cherni_Vrah] , calling it a "hike for a more just Bulgaria". [LINK: ([77]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-78]  Radev and his running mate, Iotova, highlighted that they wished to run a positive campaign based on reflecting "the voice of Bulgarian citizens". [LINK: ([78]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-79]  [LINK: ([79]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-80]  During the campaign, Radev managed to fundraise 730,000 Leva, double the amount fund raised by his closest opponent, Atanas Gerdjikov. [LINK: ([80]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-81]

After the first round, held on 14 November, Radev came out as the strongest candidate with 49% of the vote, which however still meant that he had to face GERB candidate Atanas Gerdjikov in the second round. [LINK: ([81]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-82]  In his statement after the election result, Radev characterised his victory as a "break from corruption, lawlessness, and authoritarianism", and claimed that attempts to "divide and manipulate society" had failed. Radev called on voters to not become apathetic, claiming that they were facing the "organised forces of the status quo" in the second round. [LINK: ([82]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-83]  According to Gallup, Radev voters came primarily from PP, BSP and ITN. [LINK: ([83]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-84]

In the aftermath of the first round, the two leading candidates agreed to hold a debate. [LINK: ([84]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-85]  The debate was held on the state broadcaster,  [LINK: (BNT) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_National_Television] , and lasted 90 minutes. [LINK: ([85]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-86]  During the debate, the two candidates mostly agreed on the need for a regular cabinet, continuing the current policy towards North Macedonia, as well as the need for vaccinations against the COVID-19 pandemic. [LINK: ([86]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-87]  The two candidates ended up clashing on the topic of refugees, with Gerdjikov being open to providing asylum for some refugees, while Radev stood against, as well as the caretaker cabinets' handling of the COVID pandemic, with Radev defending it, while Gerdjikov criticised it. [LINK: ([87]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-88]  The most controversial topic of the debate came during discussions of  [LINK: (Bulgaria-Russia relations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgaria-Russia_relations] , with Gerdjikov arguing for continued sanctions, while Radev called the current sanctions ineffective. To the question of "Who does the Crimean Peninsula belong to?", Radev gave a controversial answer saying "Right now, Russia", which caused outrage in Ukraine. [LINK: ([88]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-89]  Radev subsequently insisted that his words reflected the current territorial reality, and not his view on Crimea's status within international law. [LINK: ([89]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-90]

In the second round, held on 21 November, Rumen Radev defeated Gerdjikov with 66% of the vote, thus securing a second term in office. [LINK: ([90]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-91]  In his victory speech, Radev thanked those who had supported him and underlined the fact that his election proved that citizens do not wish a return of the "status-quo", despite the strong campaign for it, instead supporting a program of change. [LINK: ([91]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-92]  The election of Radev was hailed as a major victory by PP co-leader, Kiril Petkov, saying that "we will continue the change with President Radev". [LINK: ([92]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-93]  Radev was also congratulated by BSP leader,  [LINK: (Korneliya Ninova) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korneliya_Ninova] , who highlighted the overwhelming support for President Radev. Other political figures, notably  [LINK: (Revival) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Revival_(Bulgarian_political_party)]  leader  [LINK: (Konstadin Kostadinov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kostadin_Kostadinov_(politician)]  [LINK: ([93]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-94]  and GERB leader  [LINK: (Boyko Borisov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boyko_Borisov] , [LINK: ([94]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-95]  highlighted the low turnout, with this being the lowest turnout for a second round of a presidential election since the end of communism in 1990. Radev officially began his second term on 22 January 2022, with the inauguration ceremony taking place in front of the "Dondukov-2" Palace—the workplace of the president. In his inauguration address, President Radev highlighted the struggle against corruption and lawlessness and promised to continue fighting against them in his new term. [LINK: ([95]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-96]

After the  [LINK: (2021 November parliamentary election) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_Bulgarian_general_election] , Radev endorsed the formation of a "government of change". [LINK: ([96]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-97]  This was treated as an endorsement of a government led by the recently created party  [LINK: (PP) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/We_Continue_the_Change] , which was led by  [LINK: (Kiril Petkov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kiril_Petkov]  and  [LINK: (Assen Vassilev) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assen_Vassilev] , two people who held positions in the Radev appointed  [LINK: (First Yanev Government) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Yanev_Government] . On 6 December, President Radev gave the first exploratory mandate for government formation to the leaders of PP, Kiril Petkov and Assen Vassilev, asking them to form a "diverse", "non-personalist" cabinet. [LINK: ([97]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-98]  The mandate was returned to the President as completed on 11 December, with the announcement of the  [LINK: (Petkov Government) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petkov_Government] . Radev underlined the "responsibility" of the Petkov government to "reform the broken political system and fight corruption, illegality, inequality and the forces of the  [LINK: (deep state) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deep_state] ". [LINK: ([98]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-99]

Despite the early support, Radev became increasingly critical of the Petkov government. On 1 February 2022, Radev released a statement to the press in which he criticised the government's approach to North Macedonia, calling Petkov's visit to  [LINK: (Skopje) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skopje]  "too soon". [LINK: ([99]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-100]  He also criticised Petkov's decision to change the board of directors of the state company  [LINK: (Bulgargaz) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgargaz] , claiming it was "reminiscent of the style of governance of  [LINK: (Boyko Borissov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boyko_Borisov] ". [LINK: ([100]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-101]  Later, on 17 February, Radev quipped that "it is not enough to have good intentions- it is also important to take practical measures" when talking about the government's response to increased inflation, noting that in his view, "the new persons responsible for energy policy, appointed by the new government, have not achieved the necessary results". [LINK: ([101]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-102]  Despite the increasingly harsh criticism from President Radev, Prime Minister Petkov continued to insist that dialogue between the two figures was "constructive". [LINK: ([102]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-103]

The conflict between the PM and President intensified after the  [LINK: (Russian invasion of Ukraine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine] , with President Radev opposing the imposition of sanctions and the sending of lethal aid, while Kiril Petkov was more open to it. [LINK: ([103]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-104]  Radev was also critical of attempts by the government to reshuffle Bulgaria's security services. For example, after Prime Minister Petkov was attacked with snowballs during a speech on  [LINK: (Mount Shipka) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shipka_Pass]  for  [LINK: (Bulgaria's Liberation day) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberation_Day_(Bulgaria)]  (3 March), President Radev dismissed calls to change the leadership of key security services because the incident did not constitute "a breach of security". [LINK: ([104]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-105]

In April, the government requested that President Radev remove Gen. Emil Tonev as head of the National Security Service, which is responsible for the protection of government officials and government buildings. [LINK: ([105]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-106]  The reasoning given was that Tonev had transferred video tapes collected by cameras at the entrances of the Ministerial Council to the General Prosecutors office, without informing the prime minister. While Radev criticised the request for video tapes by the General Prosecutors Office, he refused to remove Gen. Emil Tonev from his position, citing the motivation as "surface-level and unreasonable" and accusing the Petkov government of trying to "politicise the security services". [LINK: ([106]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-107]

On 27 April the Russian state company  [LINK: (Gazprom) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gazprom]  announced that it would cease deliveries to Bulgaria, after Bulgaria refused to pay in Rubles. [LINK: ([107]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-108]  Radev criticised the government, claiming that the government's aggressive foreign policy approach, including state visits to Kyiv, imperilled energy security and led to higher inflation. [LINK: ([108]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-109]

On 17 June, a proposal was submitted by France which aimed to create a "framework for negotiations" between  [LINK: (North Macedonia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Macedonia]  and Bulgaria, with Bulgaria lifting the veto on  [LINK: (accession of North Macedonia to the European Union) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accession_of_North_Macedonia_to_the_European_Union] , in exchange for North Macedonia's government's commitment toward protecting the  [LINK: (Bulgarian minority in North Macedonia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarians_in_North_Macedonia] . [LINK: ([109]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-110]  President Radev was critical of the French proposal, and criticised the Petkov government for not "taking a clear position on it" and instead "transferring responsibility to the  [LINK: (National Assembly) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_National_Assembly] ". [LINK: ([110]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-111]  Petkov, in turn, criticised Radev for not calling a Consultative Council on National Security to discuss the "French proposal". [LINK: ([111]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-112]

Despite his continued criticisms of the government, Radev refrained from commenting on the vote of no confidence against the Petkov government, calling it a "routine moment in a democratic society". [LINK: ([112]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-113]  Simultaneously, Radev underlined his readiness to form a caretaker cabinet and called on MPs to "refrain from becoming political nomads". [LINK: ([113]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-114]  After the successful vote of no confidence, Radev began the process of government formation. On 28 July, the  [LINK: (BSP) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_Socialist_Party]  leader  [LINK: (Korneliya Ninova) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korneliya_Ninova]  returned the third mandate incomplete, thus meaning that new elections were to be scheduled. In her statement after the return of the third mandate, Ninova claimed that Radev was "part of the group" which had removed the cabinet from power. [LINK: ([114]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-115]  Due to the failure of government formation, early elections were called for October, and a  [LINK: (caretaker cabinet) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Donev_Government]  headed by Radev's social policy advisor,  [LINK: (Galab Donev) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galab_Donev]  was appointed.

On 2 August, Radev appointed a caretaker cabinet headed by Galab Donev. [LINK: ([115]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-116]  Radev was criticised by the BSP for the appointment of BSP members in the cabinet, with Ninova insisting that the party would not take responsibility for their actions and that this was an attempt by Radev to meddle in the internal affairs of the party. [LINK: ([116]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-117]  Ninova went as far as to say that the new government was "revanchist" against BSP. [LINK: ([117]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-118]  In his official presentation of the caretaker cabinet, Radev outlined its goals as being to rein in "the many crises facing our country" and reduce the possibility of Bulgaria's involvement in an armed conflict. [LINK: ([118]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-119]  With the appointment of Galab Donev, Radev became the first president in Bulgarian history to appoint 3 caretaker prime ministers in his tenure.

The snap elections, which took place on 3 October, once again produced a fragmented parliament, with no single party being the obvious favourite in terms of government formation. Addressing the opening session of the newly elected 48th National Assembly on 19 October, Radev expressed hope that it would finally bring the country out of political deadlock and form a regular cabinet, while also cautioning MPs away from entanglement in foreign wars. [LINK: ([119]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-120]  In the weeks following the gathering of parliament, Radev postponed the handing of the first mandate to GERB—the winning party—citing the fact that he wished to grant more time for negotiations and wished to avoid holding a snap election during winter. [LINK: ([120]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-121]    Some in the media accused Radev of postponing the government formation process in order to keep the Donev caretaker cabinet in power for a longer period of time. [LINK: ([121]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-122]

Radev handed the first mandate for government formation to GERB on 2 December, with GERB nominating neuro-seurgeon Nikolai Gabrovski, as their prime ministerial candidate. [LINK: ([122]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-123]   The cabinet failed its investiture vote on 14 December, with Radev seeing the main reason for its failure in the fact that GERB had bet on "peoples amnesia" about their period in government, which had not taken place. [LINK: ([123]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-124]  He further confirmed that he would only give the second mandate to PP after the New Year. [LINK: ([124]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-125]

On 14 December, Radev placed his veto on a controversial new law, which would have amended the electoral code of the country to remove the option to vote using newly installed machine-voting systems. [LINK: ([125]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-126]  On 2 January 2023, Radev gave the second mandate to PP as the second largest parliamentary group. [LINK: ([126]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-127]  However, the second mandate was not realized, and was returned unfulfilled on 9 January, by PP prime ministerial candidate  [LINK: (Nikolai Denkov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nikolai_Denkov] . During the return, Radev advised PP to consider negotiations with GERB in order to form a regular cabinet. [LINK: ([127]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-128]

There was much speculation about who would receive the third mandate, with analysts most commonly mentioning the Bulgarian Socialist Party (which had received the third mandate the previous times), Bulgaria, Arise! (a party seen as close to Radev, due to its leader being the former PM of a caretaker government appointed by Radev) and Democratic Bulgaria (who had been endorsed by PP and GERB). Radev gave the third mandate to BSP on 16 January, a decision which was read by some analysts as being an indicator that Radev wished to force new elections, due to BSP's poor negotiating position, and as part of an appeal to leftists. [LINK: ([128]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-129]  [LINK: ([129]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-130]  After the return of the third mandate unfulfilled on 21 January, which made new elections inevitable, Radev described the third mandate as "initially doomed" due to "a lack of will from political parties to govern during a time of crisis". [LINK: ([130]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-131]

On 3 February, Radev reappointed Donev as caretaker prime minister, after the Bulgarian Parliament once again failed to form a regular government. In the presentation of the renewed caretaker cabinet, which had the same composition sans the minister of culture, he praised them for "stopping the free fall of the state", reining in inflation and controlling energy prices, while also claiming that "the political parties will try to gain credit for the successes of the caretaker cabinet". [LINK: ([131]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-132]  Radev set reducing poverty and insuring stable levels of CPI as the two main goals of the reappointed caretaker government. The tenure of Galab Donev's second caretaker cabinet ended on 6 June 2023, with the election of the Denkov cabinet. During the transfer of power, Radev praised the caretaker cabinet for their ability to rein in the various crises afflicting Bulgaria, specifically by lowering fuel prices. [LINK: ([132]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-133]

Throughout the period of the Donev caretaker cabinet, Radev often exchanged mutual criticisms with political parties represented in the Bulgarian parliament, most commonly with  [LINK: (PP) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/We_Continue_the_Change]  and  [LINK: (DB) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democratic_Bulgaria] . During government formation consultations, held in November 2022, Radev attacked DB for their support of sending lethal aid to Ukraine, claiming it was "unreasonable" to send weapons systems before rearmament. [LINK: ([133]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-134]  On 4 December, PP co-leader and former Finance Minister Assen Vassilev, accused President Radev of "regular meetings" with former Prime Minister and GERB leader, Boyko Borisov, noting the "level of coordination" between the caretaker cabinet and GERB. [LINK: ([134]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-135]  Vasilev further called Radev the "chief reason" for Bulgaria's non-ascension into the Schengen Area. In response, Radev sharply criticised Vassilev, claiming that this "was not the first time he had lied" and expressed regret about his appointment as caretaker finance minister. [LINK: ([135]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-136]

During the election campaign for the  [LINK: (April 2023 Bulgarian parliamentary election) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2023_Bulgarian_parliamentary_election] , BSP consistently accused Radev of allegedly meddling in internal party affairs. [LINK: ([136]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-137]  [LINK: ([137]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-138]  BSP sent an official complain to the OSCE and PACE alleging illegal meddling by the caretaker government and president in their internal politics, as well as the election campaign after an interview by justice minister Krum Zarkov criticising the party leadership. [LINK: ([138]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-139]  The  [LINK: (PP–DB coalition) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PP%E2%80%93DB]  also accused the president of meddling after Radev called them "the parties of war", referring to their support of sending arms to Ukraine. [LINK: ([139]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-140]  [LINK: ([140]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-141]  Radev's statement led key DB figures to call for his impeachment. [LINK: ([141]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-142]  During his address on 3 March ( [LINK: (Bulgaria's Liberation day) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberation_Day_(Bulgaria)] ), Radev criticised "those who wish to burn Bulgaria in the fires of war" and "who wish to disarm Bulgaria at a time of war, who are ready to abandon our fellow citizens near Vardar, and who wish to turn the Republic into a cheap political theater". [LINK: ([142]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-143]

After the parliamentary elections, held on 3 April, Radev announced his decision to convene the new 49th National Assembly on 12 April. [LINK: ([143]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-144]  During the opening session, Radev gave a speech calling for parties to put aside their differences and engage in dialogue in order to "leave the political crisis" and "defend our European pathway, as well as our future as a democratic society". [LINK: ([144]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-145]  Shortly after the convocation of the 49th National Assembly, Radev re-appointed Plamen Tonchev to a second term as head of the  [LINK: (State Agency for National Security) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/State_Agency_for_National_Security] . [LINK: ([145]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-146]  In the following week, Radev held consultations with the parliamentary groups to explore avenues for government formation: during these meetings, the largest party, GERB, expressed a willingness to govern together with PP–DB; however, PP–DB continued to insist that they would not govern with GERB. [LINK: ([146]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-147]  Following the consultations, he decided to postpone the handing of the first mandate to GERB in order to enable the parliament to pass laws—specifically in relation to the budget, EU Plan for Recovery and Sustainability, as well as Bulgaria's ascension into the Schengen area. [LINK: ([147]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-148]  During  [LINK: (Europe Day) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Europe_Day]  celebrations, Radev announced that he would hand GERB the first exploratory mandate on 15 May, with Radev criticising the parliament for not doing enough to pass important laws. [LINK: ([148]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-149]  On 15 May, Radev gave the first mandate to GERB's prime ministerial candidate,  Mariya Gabriel, wishing her luck in forming a government. [LINK: ([149]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-150]  On 22 May, GERB PM candidate, Mariya Gabriel, returned the first mandate to President Radev, citing the fact that an agreement was not reached in parliament. [LINK: ([150]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-151]

Radev was scheduled to hand the second mandate to PP–DB PM candidate,  [LINK: (Nikolay Denkov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nikolai_Denkov] , on 29 May. A few days prior, an audio tape was leaked by MP Radostin Vasilev, allegedly from the meeting of the PP National Council during a discussion on governing with GERB. This tape  seemed to insinuate that PP leaders had made a deal with Radev to win the  [LINK: (2021 Bulgarian general election) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_Bulgarian_general_election] , wished to make changes within the security services, which they claimed were controlled by "Radev's people"  and suspected that Radev was planning to enter politics in the near future. [LINK: ([151]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-152]  Radev dismissed these accusations, highlighting that "the security services don't belong to the President, but to Bulgaria as a whole". [LINK: ([152]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-153]

When giving the second mandate to Denkov as PP–DB's  prime ministerial candidate on 29 May, Radev called on Denkov to return the mandate unfulfilled because it was "discredited" due to the release of the audio tapes. [LINK: ([153]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-154]  Radev's statement led to the organisation of protests by PPDB and other organisations outside the presidential building calling for Radev's impeachment due to his alleged breach of his constitutional duties as well as condemning his allegedly pro-Russian views. [LINK: ([154]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-155]  Visiting the summit of the  [LINK: (European Political Community) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Political_Community]  on 1 June, Radev continued to criticise the proposed Denkov–Gabriel government, declaring that he did not expect "the kiss between Borisov and Petkov to produce anything but disgust, and certainly not trust or development", further claiming that the leaked audiotapes proved the undemocratic intentions of the incoming government. [LINK: ([155]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-156]

On 5 June, Denkov returned the second exploratory mandate completed, with Radev promising to submit the appointment of the Denkov government to a vote by the National Assembly within the shortest time frame. [LINK: ([156]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-157]  The vote of investiture in Denkov's government took place on 6 June, being personally attended by President Radev, who when entering the National Assembly told the media he hoped that MPs would vote against the proposed cabinet if they "were not partisan servants", and expressed hope that if elected the "newly elected government will not betray the national interest, just as the leaders of the political parties (PP/DB and GERB) had betrayed the interests of their voters". [LINK: ([157]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-158]   Upon the successful investiture of the government, President Radev demonstratively left the session, saying that the government was "formed with deep compromises to the essence of democracy". [LINK: ([158]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-159]

President Radev condemned the alleged assassination of General Prosecutor  [LINK: (Ivan Geshev) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ivan_Geshev]  on 1 May as "an attack against Bulgarian institutions". [LINK: ([159]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-160]  As divisions appeared within the Prosecutors Office, Radev refused to comment. He responded with scepticism to calls by GERB prime ministerial candidate, Mariya Gabriel, for the removal of Ivan Geshev, citing the fact that those nominated by GERB in the Supreme Judicial Council had previously voted in favour of Geshev. [LINK: ([160]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-161]  On 18 May, Radev reiterated his position that Ivan Geshev should resign; however, he pointed out that the problems within Bulgaria's judicial system were not the responsibility of a single person. [LINK: ([161]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-162]

Some media outlets accused Radev of attempting to "save" Geshev, by not signing a newly passed law which would have lowered the number of votes necessary in order to remove the General Prosecutor a few days before the vote on Geshev's future was to take place. [LINK: ([162]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-163]  However, he was quick to sign into law the removal of Ivan Geshev from his position as general prosecutor on 15 June, only a few days after the vote had passed within the SJC. [LINK: ([163]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-164]  A few days after the removal of Ivan Geshev, and the speedy emplacement of his deputy, Borislav Serafov as acting general prosecutor, Radev claimed that the political parties "planned to put their own man as general prosecutor" and called on civil society to "keep tracking and exercise control over the activities of the General Prosecutor". [LINK: ([164]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-165]

After the successful investiture of the  [LINK: (Denkov Government) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denkov_Government] , Radev continued to criticise it throughout its tenure. On 23 June, Radev criticised the government for not being cautious enough to avoid Bulgarian "involvement" in the  [LINK: (Russo-Ukrainian War) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine] , and called on the Bulgarian government to "focus more on our own army, rather than foreign ones". [LINK: ([165]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-166]  Radev further stated that he, as the nominal commander-in-chief of the Bulgarian Armed Forces should have greater involvement in determining the foreign policy direction of the country. [LINK: ([166]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-167]

On 10 July, shortly after a controversial meeting with Zelensky, Radev criticised the current government's economic and foreign policy- believing that "Bulgaria should not join the Eurozone at any cost, but only when it is ready" and condemning the transfer of military aid to Ukraine. [LINK: ([167]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-168]  On 18 July, following protests which called for Radev's resignation in Sofia due to his alleged pro-Russian views, Radev accused the current government of "hiding behind the topic of Ukraine" and called on them to "focus on issues within Bulgaria". [LINK: ([168]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-169]  On 19 July, Radev criticised the current government, specifically  [LINK: (Yes, Bulgaria!) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yes,_Bulgaria!]  leader,  [LINK: (Hristo Ivanov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hristo_Ivanov_(politician)]  for "betraying his values and voters" by "not just embracing the 'deep state' but serving them", he further called on the Bulgarian government to do more to "protect Bulgarian interests"—including putting greater pressure on North Macedonia to amend its constitution to include Bulgarians and taking a clear position on the recently updated  [LINK: (Myrotvorets) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Myrotvorets]  list which included Bulgarian politicians. [LINK: ([169]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-170]  He condemned plans by the Denkov government to end the concession of the refinery Rosenets to the Russian state-owned oil company  [LINK: (Lukoil) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lukoil] , calling the announced plans "either in service of corporate appetites or crisis-PR". [LINK: ([170]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-171]

On 26 July, with the release of proposed constitutional amendments by the PPDB parliamentary group, Radev released a statement describing the proposed amendments as "legally illeterate and politically untenable"—specifically criticising the "attack on local government institutions and the presidency", as well as the change of the "National Holiday", from 3 March (Liberation Day) to 24 May (Day of Enlightenment and the National Language). [LINK: ([171]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-172]  After the joint project for constitutional changes was submitted by the PPDB, DPS, GERB-SDS parliamentary groups, Radev called it "a part of an offensive against democracy". [LINK: ([172]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-173]

Radev, commenting the assassination of Bulgarian businessman  [LINK: (Aleksei Petrov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aleksei_Petrov_(businessman)] , accused the government of "trying to use its failures in order to gain more power" and dismissed the idea of holding a "Consultative Council of National Security" due to a lack of faith in the integrity of figures in the Denkov government. [LINK: ([173]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-174]  On 24 August, Radev received a request from the Minister of Interior, Kalin Stoyanov, to remove the secretary of the minister of interior, Petar Todorov. Radev refused to sign the dismissal, stating that the motivations for Todorov's dismissal were "contradicted by evidence from the Ministry of Interior statistics" and "part of a wide-scale purge within the state institutions". [LINK: ([174]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-175]  On 30 August, Radev submitted an official rejection of Stoyanov's request to remove Petar Todorov, [LINK: ([175]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-176]  however, Todorov resigned a few hours later. [LINK: ([176]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-177]

On 27 August, Radev responded to statements made by PP co-leader Kiril Petkov who demanded that GERB leader Boyko Borisov "choose whether he wishes to work with the President or to reform the security services", by highlighting the role of both parties in supporting the present government, and further lamenting that "in this situation, logic seems to disappear more and more often". [LINK: ([177]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-178]  During a visit to the village of Nedelino, on 1 September, Radev said Borisov's recent admission that assassinated businessman Petrov had a role in the formation of the Denkov government was "an admission that the country is governed by the 'deep state'". [LINK: ([178]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-179]

On 11 September, following an announcement by PPDB of their intention to remove most of the current security service heads within the country, Radev criticised the statement saying that "reforming the security services doesn't just mean placing 'your people' in charge". [LINK: ([179]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-180]  After the revelation that at least three priests of the Russian Orthodox Church in Bulgaria were working as spies, he defended the work of DANS against criticism by PPDB and refused to allow for the removal of its current chief. [LINK: ([180]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-181]  On 18 October, during the Day of Bulgarian Aviation, Radev accused the government of ruling with authoritarian intentions and denied accusations that he had "declared war" on the government, instead saying they had started a campaign against him. [LINK: ([181]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-182]  He further accused the government of trying to secretly sell the oil refinery in Burgas. [LINK: ([182]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-183]

After the local elections, Radev refused to dismiss the chief of DANS, who the government said had authorised the release of  misleading report made by one of his deputies, which had undermined trust in the electoral system. [LINK: ([183]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-184]  He criticised the government's decision to impose the tax on Gazprom, pointing out that it led to a negative reaction by Hungary's government. [LINK: ([184]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-185]  Radev condemned the planned removal of the  [LINK: (Monument to the Soviet Army, Sofia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monument_to_the_Soviet_Army,_Sofia] , calling it an "aggressive offensive against statehood, history and memory". [LINK: ([185]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-186]

Before leaving on a two-day trip to the Republic of Kosovo, on 22 December, Radev discussed the constitutional amendments passed by the National Assembly, criticing PPDB for "only recently claiming Borisov and Peevski are involved in corruption and in the 'deep state' but now they govern together. Rape the constitution together ...", further saying that the government must ensure a clear time frame for Bulgaria's entry into the Schengen Area be provided before 2024. [LINK: ([186]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-187]

During the traditional New Years Address, Radev lamented that the future development of Bulgaria as a "free European democracy" was impossible in an atmosphere of "war with national symbols and historical memory",  "political hypocrisy" and "tolerance for corruption". [LINK: ([187]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-188]  While inspecting the Bulgarian Armed Forces on 6 January, Radev stated that "entering 2024 we come with mixed feeling after a year of half-measures" and claimed that 2024 will be "the year of choices", not just for the EU Parliament, but "for Bulgaria's future". [LINK: ([188]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-189]  Commenting the upcoming rotation of the Prime Ministers position, Radev characterised the incumbent ministers as "pawns". [LINK: ([189]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-190]

During the commemoration of  [LINK: (Vasil Levski) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vasil_Levski] 's death on 19 February, Radev said that Levski's ideals were more necessary than ever in a time of "the rotation of principles", which was seen as a criticism of the Denkov government. [LINK: ([190]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-191]  In the following days, Radev continued to criticise the government's alleged "lack of principles", although he made clear that he would not impede the upcoming rotation and would "strictly follow the Constitution". [LINK: ([191]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-192]

As part of the celebration dedicated to the 100 year anniversary of the  [LINK: (Ilinden Uprising) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ilinden%E2%80%93Preobrazhenie_Uprising] , Radev gave a speech on top of  [LINK: (Mount Shipka) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shipka_Pass]  on 19 August. In the address, he accused the current political class of attempting to "erase" Bulgarian historical memory, due to "their false belief that to be European we must reject the fact we are Bulgarians". [LINK: ([192]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-193]  He further accused the Bulgarian government of authoritarianism due to the proposed constitutional changes. Radev announced that "3 March is the red line of our patience"—in reference to the government's plans to change the National Holiday from the  [LINK: (3 March) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberation_Day_(Bulgaria)]  (the  [LINK: (Liberation of Bulgaria) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberation_of_Bulgaria] ) to  [LINK: (24 May) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Day_of_Slavonic_Alphabet,_Bulgarian_Enlightenment_and_Culture] —the day of the  [LINK: (Slavonic alphabet) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Early_Cyrillic_alphabet] . He went on to declare that "today we put an end to the countdown, to the dissolution and declare that Bulgaria will remain a Republic of equal and free citizens". Throughout the speech, Radev referred to a "people's movement"—specifically, in his final line he declared that "the people's movement will defend 3 March". [LINK: ([193]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-194]

A number of political analysts at the time speculated that this meant the beginning of Radev's open participation in Bulgarian electoral politics. [LINK: ([194]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-195]  [LINK: ([195]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-196]  [LINK: ([196]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-197]  Such speculation intensified after the creation of an initiative committee to hold a referendum for the preservation of Third of March as the national holiday, which was joined by a number of people close to the president, including presidential adviser Alexander Marinov. [LINK: ([197]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-198]  However, organisers of the committee, including MEP Petar Vitanov, insisted that the initiative committee did not involve the president and was not intended as the basis for a future political party. [LINK: ([198]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-199]

On 29 January 2024, when commenting on the prospects of him potentially founding a political party, Radev said that "the rise of an alternative political force" was inevitable due to the failings of the current government and political parties. [LINK: ([199]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-200]  Later however, Radev took a step to cool rumours about his involvement in politics, denying his involvement with the "Third of March" movement and stating that if he supported a party he would make that support public. [LINK: ([200]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-201]  Speaking on Mount Shipka as part of the Liberation Day celebrations on the Third of March, Radev thanked those who had gathered and creditted them with "defeating" the government effort to change the date of the National Holiday. [LINK: ([201]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-202]

Оn 14 June, Radev vetoed the recently passed "Law on the Judicial Power", which would have separated the investigative branch from the competency of the General Prosecutor, citing the fact that such a system had existed prior to 2009 and was replaced in order to adhere to EU norms. [LINK: ([202]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-203]   The veto was supported by the parliament, with the law being therefore withdrawn. [LINK: ([203]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-204]  On 11 July, Radev submitted an official complaint to the  [LINK: (Constitutional Court) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constitutional_Court_of_Bulgaria]  wherein he disputed the appointment of  [LINK: (Goritsa Grancharova) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goritsa_Grancharova]  as acting chief of the  [LINK: (Chamber of Audit) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chamber_of_Audit_(Bulgaria)] , citing to the fact that the previous chief Tsvetkov's dismissal by the National Assembly had been deemed illegal by the Constitutional Court in a previous ruling. [LINK: ([204]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-205]  The Constitutional Court ruled in Radev's favour, invalidating all acts made by the Chamber of Audit during her time as acting chief. [LINK: ([205]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-206]

On 1 August, Radev put a veto on the amendments to the "Law on Judicial Powers", motivating his veto by claiming that the new changes would be overly disruptive to the newly created process of investigating the General Prosecutor. [LINK: ([206]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-207]  The veto was overturned by the National Assembly. On 4 August, Radev submitted a challenge to the legality of the decision to cease the concession of the oil refinery at Rosenets before the Constitutional Court, citing irregularities within the law and a lack of clarity about how the refinery was to be managed. [LINK: ([207]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-208]

In October, Radev challenged two more laws in the Constitutional Court. He challenged the Law on the Preservation of Agricultural Land, due to its enabling of expanded construction of renewable energy sources on high productivity agricultural land. [LINK: ([208]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-209]   He also challenged the decision of the government to impose a tax on gas passing through the Gazprom pipelines located in Bulgaria, on the grounds it violated the right to private property. [LINK: ([209]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-210]  Radev's decision to challenge the tax led to criticism from PM Denkov, and his finance minister, Assen Vassilev, who accused him of serving Russian interests by challenging the tax. [LINK: ([210]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-211]  Radev also vetoed the newly passed "Energy Law", citing the fact that the liberalization of the energy market would negatively impact Bulgarian lower-income consumers. [LINK: ([211]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-212]   The veto ended up being overturned by the parliament on 10 November.

On 4 December, Radev vetoed the transfer of 100 Bulgarian APCs to Ukraine, saying they could be used by the Bulgarian Ministry of Interior in cases of natural emergencies and to protect the border with Turkey. [LINK: ([212]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-213]  The veto was overturned by the parliament on 8 December. During the work on the budget for 2024, Radev threatened to veto it due to it incurring new debts and potentially involving the alleged sale of public land. [LINK: ([213]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-214]  Upon the passing of the constitutional changes proposed by the government on 20 December, President Radev promised to challenge them before the Constitutional Court, claiming that the changes would "undermine the democracy, legality and sovereignty of the state". [LINK: ([214]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-215]

On 8 January 2024, Radev officially submitted his challenge of the Constitutional Amendments to the Constitutional Court, which focused on three major areas within the new constitutional changes: the alleged changes within the balance of powers which could only be authorised by a  [LINK: (Grand National Assembly) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_National_Assembly_of_Bulgaria] , alleged contradictions with other sections of the constitution, the alleged non-compliance with the time-frame for the debate of constitutional amendments set by the Constitution. [LINK: ([215]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-216]  On 23 January, Radev submitted his challenge of the election of two new Justice by the National Assembly to fill the vacancies within the Constitutional Court, claiming that their election violated the constitution by not following the proper procedures and not being transparent enough. [LINK: ([216]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-217]  Radev went on to not attend the official swearing in ceremony of the elected Constitutional Judges, which may disrupt their selection to the Constitutional Court since the Constitution states they must place their oath in the presence of the president. [LINK: ([217]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-218]  On 12 February, Radev vetoed an amendments to the Punishment-Procedural Code, which would have revived the recently abolished Supreme Judicial Council (SJC) for an indeterminate period of time, due to the fact that this amendment contradicted the recently approved amendments to the Constitution which had liquidated the institution and split it into two separate councils (one for judges, one for prosecutors). [LINK: ([218]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-219]  The veto ended up being upheld by the Bulgarian National Assembly. [LINK: ([219]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-220]  On 29 April, he vetoed the "Investment Promotion Act" due to it decreasing environmental protections and reducing the time limits of Environmental Impact Assessments. [LINK: ([220]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-221]

On 5 March, Prime Minister Nikolay Denkov resigned as part of the previously agreed governance deal with GERB, which foresaw the minister of foreign affairs,  [LINK: (Mariya Gabriel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mariya_Gabriel]  assuming the position. [LINK: ([221]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-222]  Following Denkov's resignation, Radev assumed his constitutional duty within government formations by first summoning the Parliamentary Groups for consultations. [LINK: ([222]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-223]  During the consultation, the representatives of  [LINK: (GERB-SDS) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GERB-SDS] , headed by Mariya Gabriel, informed Radev about the ongoing negotiations with their governing partners, PPDB, and requested a time extension for continued negotiations. [LINK: ([223]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-224]   [LINK: (PP-DB) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PP-DB]  were asked by Radev about their relationship with GERB-SDS, with PPDB confirming their intention to form a government together with GERB. [LINK: ([224]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-225]   [LINK: (DPS) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Movement_for_Rights_and_Freedoms] , the third largest Parliamentary Group, confirmed they would continue to give support to the government, however did not exclude the possibility of creating a new governing majority within the National Assembly. [LINK: ([225]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-226]  All the other parties involved in the consultations (Revival, BSP and ITN) expressed their support for early elections. [LINK: ([226]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-227]

Following the consultations, Radev scheduled the handing of the first mandate to the largest Parliamentary Group, GERB-SDS, for 15 March. [LINK: ([227]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-228]  However, the ceremony ended up being postponed to Monday, 18 March, due to the death of the  [LINK: (Patriarch) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patriarch_of_All_Bulgaria]  of the  [LINK: (Bulgarian Orthodox Church) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_Orthodox_Church] ,  [LINK: (Neophyte) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neophyte_of_Bulgaria]  and the subsequent period of mourning. [LINK: ([228]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-229]  On 18 March, Radev officially gave the first mandate to the GERB-SDS PM-candidate, Mariya Gabriel, in the presence of a mixed delegation of GERB and PPDB MPs, as well as the acting prime minister Nikolay Denkov. [LINK: ([229]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-230]  Despite having  seven days for negotiations, Mariya Gabriel decided to return the first mandate completed to President Radev at 15:00 on 19 March. [LINK: ([230]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-231]  Gabriel officially presented her cabinet to Radev, which was similar in composition to the outgoing  [LINK: (Denkov Government) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denkov_Government] , however had a number of changes, specifically the Defense Minister and Energy Minister. Shortly thereafter, Radev published a decree proposing the candidacy of Gabriel to the National Assembly. [LINK: ([231]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-232]

Subsequently, a number of the proposed-ministers in the proposed Gabriel government,  announced that they did not consent to their participation in the proposed government, leading PPDB to call for President Radev to not present the Mariya Gabriel government for a vote due to its potential unconstitutionality. [LINK: ([232]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-233]  Radev, however, refused on the grounds that according to the Constitution the president only presents a nominee for prime minister, and is not responsible for the composition of the Cabinet. [LINK: ([233]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-234]  On 24 March, Mariya Gabriel announced the end of government negotiations with PPDB and the withdrawal of her candidacy as a candidate for PM. [LINK: ([234]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-235]  Subsequently, on the 25th, the National Assembly approved Gabriel's withdrawal as candidate for prime minister, thus voiding the first mandate and leading Radev to hand the second mandate to PPDB. [LINK: ([235]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-236]  Radev gave the second mandate to PPDB on 27 March, with PPDB instantly returning the second mandate incomplete. [LINK: ([236]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-237]  On 28 March, Radev announced that he had selected the smallest Parliamentary Group, ITN, to receive the third mandate, with ITN similarly instantly returning it to the President. [LINK: ([237]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-238]  With the return of the third mandate, the constitutionally mandated government-formation process was ended, leading to early legislative elections.

Due to the Constitutional Amendments adopted by the 49th National Assembly in December 2023, the possibilities of the president in terms of appointing a caretaker government were limited. For one, the potential caretaker PM was now restricted to a list of 10 people: the chairman of the outgoing National Assembly; the governor of the  [LINK: (Bulgarian National Bank) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_National_Bank]  and their deputies; the head of the  [LINK: (Chamber of Audit) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chamber_of_Audit_(Bulgaria)]  and their deputies; finally, the Ombudsman and their deputy. [LINK: ([238]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-239]  Additionally, the rules for the appointment of caretaker ministers remained unclear, with the president and the caretaker PM seemingly sharing responsibility for their appointment after consultations with the Parliamentary Groups. [LINK: ([239]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-240]  The Constitutional Amendments seemingly also left unclear the possibility of the potential caretaker PMs to refuse their appointment, as well as the potential possibility of a refusal to appoint a caretaker government by the president. [LINK: ([240]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-241]  Commenting the possibility of appointing a caretaker cabinet on 1 March, President Radev promised to go by the "phone book", implying that his selection of a caretaker PM would follow the significance which the positions of the potential caretaker-PM candidates have within the constitutional structure of Bulgaria. [LINK: ([241]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-242]

Upon the return of the second mandate incomplete, thus making early election a de facto inevitability, Radev lamented that the constitutional amendments supported by PP-DB had created the grounds for a "constitutional crisis" during the appointment of the caretaker cabinet, the nature of which he would "further explain" during consultations with the Parliamentary Groups. [LINK: ([242]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-243]  Radev also commented on the resignation of one of the potential caretaker-PM candidates, the deputy Ombudsman, by highlighting her nature as the only "non-political" candidate and suggesting the possibility of her resignation being forced. [LINK: ([243]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-244]  On 28 March, with the return of the third mandate, Radev officially announced that he would begin the process of the formation of a caretaker government by inviting prospective caretaker PMs for consultations. [LINK: ([244]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-245]  The meetings included the chairman of the NA,  [LINK: (Rosen Zhelyazkov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosen_Zhelyazkov] , as well as the head of the BNB,  [LINK: (Dimitar Radev) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dimitar_Radev] , and head of the Chamber of Audits, Dimitar Glavchev. [LINK: ([245]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-246]

Prior to the aforementioned meetings, GERB leader, Boyko Borisov, expressed his opposition to the appointment of either Zhelyazkov or Glavchev to the position of caretaker PM, due to their explicit connections to GERB, which Borisov argued could be used in the campaign period in order to "slander" GERB. [LINK: ([246]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-247]  Additionally, Governor of the BNB, Dimitar Radev, similarly expressed reservations about serving as caretaker PM, due to the non-political status of the BNB. [LINK: ([247]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-248]  The incumbent Ombudsman,  [LINK: (Diana Kovacheva) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diana_Kovacheva] , similarly refused the position of caretaker PM, due to her intention to become a judge at the  [LINK: (European Court of Human Rights) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Court_of_Human_Rights] . [LINK: ([248]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-249]  On 29 March, after consultations with all candidates for caretaker PM, the president Rumen Radev announced that he had selected the president of the  [LINK: (Bulgarian National Audit Office) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chamber_of_Audit_(Bulgaria)] ,  [LINK: (Dimitar Glavchev) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dimitar_Glavchev] , as caretaker PM. [LINK: ([249]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-250]

On 30 March, Radev officially invited  [LINK: (Dimitar Glavchev) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dimitar_Glavchev]  to form a caretaker government, noting that he was the only individual to not refuse the position, thus averting a constitutional crisis. [LINK: ([250]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-251]  Radev called on Glavchev to form a politically neutral cabinet and to present the cabinet before the President within a 7-day time frame, in order to insure the early elections could be scheduled together with the  [LINK: (European Elections) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_European_Parliament_election_in_Bulgaria] . During the formation and presentation of the Glavchev cabinet, Radev often reiterated that he did not have responsibility for the government's actions. He further criticised some of the choices made by Glavchev in his Cabinet composition, such as the appointment of Kalin Stoyanov as caretaker minister of interior.

Only a week after the appointment of the cabinet, Prime Minister Glavchev requested the removal of two ministers: the minister of foreign affairs, Stefan Dimitrov, and the minister of foods and agriculture, Kiril Vutev, both of whom he accused of not properly fulfilling their roles. [LINK: ([251]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-252]  In their place, he proposed GERB MP,  [LINK: (Daniel Mitov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daniel_Mitov] , for Minister of Foreign Affairs, and head of the State Fund "Agriculture",  [LINK: (Georgi Tahov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georgi_Tahov] , for Minister of Agriculture.

President Radev officially commented the proposed changes four days after their announcement, making it clear that he did not support the appointment of Mitov due to his connections to GERB, and asking PM Glavchev to meet with him in order to discuss the changes further. [LINK: ([252]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-253]  Following Glavchev's proposal for himself personally to undertake the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Radev urged him to explore alternative options, although he signalled his readiness to implement the decision if Glavchev insisted on it. [LINK: ([253]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-254]  On 22 April, Radev was expected to meet Glavchev in order to discuss the cabinet reshuffle, however Glavchev did not attend, instead submitting an official request to be appointed as caretaker minister of foreign affairs along with his current post as caretaker prime minister. [LINK: ([254]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-255]  Later that day, Radev signed the cabinet changes, thus enabling the change of two caretaker ministers. [LINK: ([255]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-256]  Radev severely criticised the decision of the Glavchev Government to approve the construction of a private children's hospital in Sofia. [LINK: ([256]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-257]

During the spring session of the  [LINK: (NATO Parliamentary Assembly) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NATO_Parliamentary_Assembly] , which was held in Bulgaria, Radev was notably absent from the country, instead attending state visit Hungary and Switzerland, during which he made a number of statements accusing the current NATO policy towards the  [LINK: (Russian invasion of Ukraine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine]  of promoting increased escalation. [LINK: ([257]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-258]  His statements led to concern within the Glavchev caretaker government, that Radev was not fit to lead the Bulgarian delegation during the upcoming NATO summit in July, which caused a dispute between the two institutions. [LINK: ([258]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-259]

On 5 June, PM Glavchev announced his intention for the parliament to decide who should lead the Bulgarian delegation, unless a regular government had been formed. [LINK: ([259]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-260]  However, the parliament on 21 June proved unable to find a consensus on the matter, leading to the cabinet's eventual decision that both Glavchev and Radev would be present at the summit. [LINK: ([260]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-actualno.com-261]  It was further decided that President Radev was to represent the delegation based on the position prepared by the cabinet. [LINK: ([260]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-actualno.com-261]  On 27 June, Radev announced that he would not represent the Bulgarian delegation citing deep disagreements with the position of the cabinet, specifically as pertains to the matter of Ukraine. [LINK: ([261]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-262]  On 9 June, Radev voted in the  [LINK: (June 2024 Bulgarian parliamentary election) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/June_2024_Bulgarian_parliamentary_election] , while voting he restored rumours about the possibility of leading a political party by noting that if the political crisis continues  the appearance of a "political alternative" is inevitable. [LINK: ([262]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-263]

Radev convened the  [LINK: (50th National Assembly) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/50th_National_Assembly_of_Bulgaria]  for 19 June. [LINK: ([263]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-264]  Unlike in previous National Assemblies, wherein the president usually made a speech during the opening session, this time Radev did not deliver a speech. [LINK: ([264]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:speech-265]  He justified his decision by stating that he had already outlined his main theses in previous speeches. [LINK: ([264]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:speech-265]  On 24 June, Radev began consultations with the parliamentary parties for the first mandate. [LINK: ([265]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-266]  During the consultations, it became clear that GERB planned to nominate a  [LINK: (minority government) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minority_government] , a move that was, however, only supported by DPS. [LINK: ([266]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-267]

He gave the first mandate to GERB's prime minister-candidate,  [LINK: (Rosen Zhelyazkov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosen_Zhelyazkov] , on 1 July, who decided to immediately complete it and submit his cabinet for a vote in the National Assembly. [LINK: ([267]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-268]  Zhelyazkov's cabinet was, however defeated in the National Assembly, thus voiding the first mandate. [LINK: ([268]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-269]  Thus, Radev was set to present the second mandate to DPS, however, an internal conflict within the party between  [LINK: (Ahmed Dogan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ahmed_Dogan]  and  [LINK: (Delyan Peevski) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delyan_Peevski]  led to a split within the parties parliamentary group, thus making PP-DB the second largest. [LINK: ([269]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-270]  On 15 July, Radev held a meeting with the representatives of PPDB to determine when they wished to receive the second mandate. [LINK: ([270]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:second-271]  During this meeting, PPDB asked the President to postpone the giving of the second mandate for three months, so that PPDB could pass key anti-corruption laws which they believed were necessary to prove that a stable majority existed. [LINK: ([270]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:second-271]   While Radev did not explicitly oppose this idea, he did point out that the primary task of PPDB was to find a governing majority, rather than legislative action. [LINK: ([270]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:second-271]  On 22 July, following the failure of PPDB's negotiations, Radev delivered the second mandate to them, with it being subsequently returned unfulfilled. [LINK: ([271]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-272]  He gave the third mandate to ITN, justifying the decision by citing their consistent positions. [LINK: ([272]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-273]  Ultimately, however, the third mandate was also returned incomplete, thus meaning new legislative elections were inevitable. [LINK: ([273]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-274]

With the failure of the government formation process, a caretaker cabinet had to be appointed. Prior to its appointment, Radev held a consultation with the Parliamentary Groups. [LINK: ([274]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:Cons-275]   During the consultation, he revealed that there were other potential candidates available for the position of caretaker PM, except the incumbent Dimitar Glavchev. [LINK: ([274]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:Cons-275]  On 9 August, Radev revealed that he had selected  [LINK: (Goritsa Grancharova) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goritsa_Grancharova] , deputy head of the Chamber of Audit, as the caretaker prime minister-designate. [LINK: ([275]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:GR-276]  He justified his decision by underlining Gracharova's non-partisan character, as well as allegations that Glavchev may be under undue political influence. [LINK: ([275]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:GR-276]  Radev gave Grancharova a 10-day period in which she was to form her cabinet, in order to hold the snap legislative elections by 20 October. [LINK: ([275]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:GR-276]

On 19 August, Grancharova officially presented her caretaker cabinet before President Radev. [LINK: ([276]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:CC-277]  The cabinet was mostly composed of Ministers from the incumbent  [LINK: (Glavchev Cabinet) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glavchev_Government] . [LINK: ([276]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:CC-277]  President Radev, however, made clear that he would not sign the decree appointing the proposed caretaker cabinet due to the controversial nomination of Kalin Stoyanov for Minister of the Interior. [LINK: ([276]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:CC-277]  Radev gave Grancharova an ultimatum to  nominate another, less controversial person to the position in the next three hours in order to avert a constitutional crisis. [LINK: ([276]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:CC-277]  Following Grancharova's refusal to comply, Radev reiterated that he would not appoint the government and appealed to the parliament that it should fill the vacancies within the list of potential caretaker prime ministers. [LINK: ([276]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:CC-277]  Radev's decision was followed by serious arguments among  [LINK: (jurists) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jurists]  about whether it was constitutional. [LINK: ([277]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-278]  On 20 August, Radev held consultations with alternative caretaker prime minister candidates in order to establish who should be selected in Grancharova's place. [LINK: ([278]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-279]

Following the consultations, Radev decided to select the incumbent caretaker prime minister, Dimitar Glavchev. [LINK: ([279]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:o02-280]  During the presentation of the mandate for caretaker government formation to Glavchev, Radev pointed out that allegations of political influence over the caretaker prime minister had emerged in recent months and that he hoped that the presentation of the new caretaker government would disprove those. [LINK: ([279]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:o02-280]  He further noted that fighting vote buying should be a key priority of the next government, implying that a change within the Ministry of Interior was necessary. [LINK: ([279]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:o02-280]  On 26 August, Glavchev presented the composition of his second caretaker government to Radev, noting the major change of  [LINK: (Kalin Stoyanov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalin_Stoyanov]  for Atanas Ilkov, in the Ministry of Interior, with Radev subsequently approving the caretaker cabinet. [LINK: ([280]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-281]

The personal conflict between Radev and disputed DPS leader, Delyan Peevski, intensified in the run-up and following the  [LINK: (October 2024 Bulgarian parliamentary election) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/October_2024_Bulgarian_parliamentary_election] . On 7 September, during a visit to the town of  [LINK: (Radnevo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radnevo] , Radev made a statement which (while not explicitly implicating Peevski) implied that his role in the state must be diminished and characterised him as an existential threat to Bulgaria's future. [LINK: ([281]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-282]  The conflict intensified following an aviation disaster at the military airfield,  [LINK: ("Graf Ignatievo") -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graf_Ignatievo_Air_Base] , wherein two pilots lost their lives. [LINK: ([282]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:Ignatievo-283]   [LINK: (DPS-Peevski) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DPS-Peevski]  leader Peevski alleged the President was responsible for the death of the servicemen due to his appointment of the current  [LINK: (air-force) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_Air_Force]  leadership. [LINK: ([282]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:Ignatievo-283]  Radev, vehemently denied the allegations made by Peevski, and accused him of attempting to extract political profit from the situation. [LINK: ([282]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:Ignatievo-283]

On 11 November, during the opening session of the 51st National Assembly, Peevski held a protest outside the Presidential residence during which he once again referred to the President as "Mr. Cash" and called him a "pseudo-president". [LINK: ([283]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-284]  In response, Radev called Peevski "shameless". [LINK: ([284]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-285]

During government consultations for the formation of a government, he pointedly did not invite the DPS - A New Beginning parliamentary group. [LINK: ([285]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-286]  Radev justified his decision by stating that DPS-NN had "self-excluded" itself from the government formation process by stating its support for new elections and further stated that the decision was fully in line with the constitution. [LINK: ([286]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-287]   The unprecedented decision led to debate about its constitutionality.

During the investiture of the Glavchev caretaker cabinet, Radev placed fighting vote-buying and other electoral law violations as its primary priority, in order to restore trust in the electoral process. However, speaking to the media on 9 October, Radev suggested that state institutions (including the caretaker cabinet) were not doing enough to prevent vote-buying, implying that a double-standard existent in treating offenders depending on political affiliation. [LINK: ([287]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-288]  When casting his ballot on 27 October, he lamented that it was vital that the integrity of the election process was insured, in order to guarantee the legitimacy of the  [LINK: (next parliament) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/51st_National_Assembly_of_Bulgaria]  and potential future governments. [LINK: ([288]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-289]

Following the publication of provisional results, Radev made a statement in which he commented on the alleged mass vote-buying allegations and urged the caretaker cabinet to publish data about the extent of vote buying and coerced voting, as well as the potential role of local state institutions in the practice. [LINK: ([289]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-290]  Speaking on 5 November, Radev claimed that if he had appointed the caretaker government, the fairness of the elections would not be in dispute. [LINK: ([290]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-291]  Commenting the convocation of the 51st National Assembly on 11 November, he stated that due to the doubts about the legitimacy of the preceding vote it was the parliament's own responsibility to prove its independence. [LINK: ([291]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-292]

The beginning of government formation procedures following the election of the 51st National Assembly was impeded by its inability to elect a Speaker, with the parliament electing Natalia Kiselova as speaker only on 6 December (almost a month following its convocation). Between 11 and 12 December, Radev held consultations with representatives of all the Parliamentary Groups, except DPS-NN. [LINK: ([292]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-293]  During the consultations, while most parties except GERB announced that they would not negotiate with DPS-NN, BSP-OL, APS, ITN and DB did not exclude the possibility of participating in government with GERB. [LINK: ([293]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-294]

Following the beginning of official negotiations between GERB and DB on December, 16th, as part of broader government formation negotiations further involving the BSP and ITN, Radev announced that the first mandate would be given to GERB after the New Year. [LINK: ([294]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-295]  Despite the seeming breakdown of government negotiations, Radev promised to grant an extended time period for the parliamentary parties before giving the first mandate to GERB. [LINK: ([295]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-296]  Ultimately, a government deal was agreed prior to the delivery of the first mandate to GERB's Prime Ministerial Candidate,  [LINK: (Rosen Zhelyazkov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosen_Zhelyazkov] , on 15 January. [LINK: ([296]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-297]  During the delivery of the first mandate, Rosen Zhelyazkov returned it fulfilled, with Radev subsequently submitting his candidacy and the composition of his cabinet to the National Assembly. [LINK: ([297]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-298]  On 16 January, the National Assembly voted to approve the  [LINK: (Zhelyazkov Government) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhelyazkov_Government] , with it subsequently being sworn in.

Commenting the investiture of the new government, Radev stated that his attitude towards the newly elected government depended on its actions, specifically urging the government to address issues faced by Bulgarians. [LINK: ([298]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-299]  In February, Radev summoned a Consultative Council for National Security in relation to reports of increased drug abuse among the youth. [LINK: ([299]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-300]  After the council, held on 18 February, it was agreed that amendments would be made to existing laws in order to increase the liability for the sale of illegal narcotics to minors and further prevent minors from having access to narcotic substances. [LINK: ([300]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-301]

Following speculation about the potential deployment of European forces in Ukraine (as part of a peace-keeping mission or deterrence force), Radev called on the National Assembly to assure Bulgarian citizens that Bulgaria would not participate in such a deployment. [LINK: ([301]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-302]  A National Assembly declaration to a similar effect was subsequently approved. On 17 February, prior to the annual commemoration of the Execution of Vasil Levski on 19 February, Radev signed a decree naming  [LINK: (Sofia Airport) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sofia_Airport]  in his honour. [LINK: ([302]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-303]  Commenting the disturbances caused by the protests of  [LINK: (Revival) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Revival_(Bulgarian_political_party)]  against the introduction of the Euro, Radev condemned the use of violence by certain protestors, however lamented that more must be done by the government in order to reduce public tensions surrounding the topic. [LINK: ([303]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-304]

Speaking at the annual Liberation Day ceremony on Mount Shipka, he called for unity and expressed hope for an end of hostilities in the Russo-Ukrainian War. [LINK: ([304]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-305]  Radev criticised state institutions in Bulgaria following an address by the Chairwoman of the Constitutional Court, Pavlina Panova, in which she stated that the work of the court when considering the case of the legitimacy of the preceding October elections was being obstructed by other state institutions. [LINK: ([305]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-306]  He further called for the re-introduction of machine voting in order to restore trust in the electoral system. [LINK: ([306]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-307]  After the Constitutional Court ruled in favour of a partial annulment of the preceding election results, Radev denied allegations by former Prime Minister and GERB leader, Boyko Borisov, that he had orchestrated the ruling. [LINK: ([307]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-308]  Speaking after the swearing-on of newly elected Constitutional Court justice, Orlin Kolev, Radev posited that the future of the Zhelyazkov Government depended on GERB and DPS-Peevski leaders, Boyko Borisov and Delyan Peevski, and criticised the incumbent government for not doing enough to pursue anti-corruption reforms. [LINK: ([308]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-309]

Speaking prior to the first vote of no confidence faced by the Zhelyazkov Government, Radev claimed that the incumbent government had attempted to avoid blame for controversial issues (such as, recent traffic accidents and the deal with Turkish energy company, Botash) by discrediting the Radev-appointed caretaker cabinets. [LINK: ([309]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-310]  He intensified his criticism of the government prior to the end of its first 100 days in office, wherein he stated that he believed that the incumbent government represented a continuation of the model of governance used by Boyko Borisov, as well as alleged corrupt practices associated with it. [LINK: ([310]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-311]

A further conflict developed between Radev, and GERB leader, Boyko Borisov, after the arrival of new  [LINK: (F-16 fighter jets) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_Dynamics_F-16_Fighting_Falcon] , which had been purchased by the  [LINK: (third Borisov government) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Third_Borisov_Government] . Borisov claimed, following reports that the first jet which had arrived suffered from technical deficiencies, that the deficiencies were the fault of head of the air force, Nikolay Rusev, who he claimed was personally responsible to President Radev. [LINK: ([311]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-312]  In turn, Radev stated that the purchase of the F-16 jets was undertaken by the Borisov government and further called on the government to take responsibility for the maintenance of the aircraft. [LINK: ([312]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-313]

In an emergency briefing, held on 9 May, President Radev announced his intention to submit a referendum about Bulgaria's ascension to the Eurozone in 2026 to the National Assembly. [LINK: ([313]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-314]  Radev was heavily criticised by the government and different political leaders in Bulgaria who branded his referendum request as unconstitutional following a ruling by the Constitutional Court in 2024 that said the referendum was not possible as ascension to the Eurozone was mandated by the Treaty on the Ascension to the European Union. [LINK: ([314]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-315]  However, other parties, such as the pro-Russian  [LINK: (Revival) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Revival_(Bulgarian_political_party)] , praised Radev's decision and claimed that its international diplomatic efforts had led to tangible results, including a shift in Radev's stance, [LINK: ([315]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-316]  with far-right parties  [LINK: (Velichie) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Velichie]  and  [LINK: (MECh) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morality,_Unity,_Honour]  also voicing support. [LINK: ([316]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-317]

Prime Minister  [LINK: (Rosen Zhelyazkov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosen_Zhelyazkov]  firmly defended Bulgaria's European path and the country's accession to the eurozone in response to Radev's call of a referendum on the euro. Zhelyazkov emphasized that  [LINK: (Bulgaria) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgaria]  had already committed to joining the eurozone through the ratification of the Treaty on Accession to the European Union. According to the Prime Minister, the  [LINK: (National Assembly) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Assembly_(Bulgaria)] , as a legitimately elected body, had made all necessary decisions regarding the adoption of the euro. [LINK: ([317]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:1-318]  GERB leader  [LINK: (Boyko Borisov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boyko_Borisov]  called on the National Assembly to swiftly reject Radev's proposal for a referendum on the euro, characterizing the move as a dangerous political stunt intent on stoking fear and division within the ruling coalition. [LINK: ([318]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-319]  The  [LINK: (BSP-United Left) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BSP_%E2%80%93_United_Left]  also criticized Radev saying a decision by the National Assembly to hold a referendum on joining the eurozone or setting specific deadlines would contravene the Constitutional Court's ruling from 8 February 2024. [LINK: ([319]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-320]  In a television interview,  [LINK: (Nikolai Denkov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nikolai_Denkov]  from  [LINK: (PP-DB) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/We_Continue_the_Change_%E2%80%93_Democratic_Bulgaria]  criticized Radev's decision, labeling it as destabilizing and unconstitutional. He stated that Radev was aware that the referendum was legally questionable and suggested that it served the interests of external forces aiming to obstruct Bulgaria's European development. Denkov asserted that the referendum should be rejected in parliament, emphasizing that it contradicted both the Constitution and the interests of Bulgaria's parliamentary majority. [LINK: ([317]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:1-318]

Borislav Tsekov, a constitutional law expert, has voiced strong opposition to Radev's referendum request on Bulgaria's adoption of the euro. Tsekov stressed to BGNES that while the President has the right to submit such a proposal, it is ultimately the National Assembly that must decide whether a referendum is held. Tsekov pointed out that the matter has already been addressed by the Constitutional Court, which has ruled against holding a referendum on the euro. Tsekov further explained that Bulgaria, by signing its EU accession agreements, has already committed to adopting the euro. The readiness of Bulgaria to join the eurozone is determined by convergence reports, and any such decision ultimately lies with the European Commission. In this context, Tsekov argued, a referendum on the euro is unconstitutional. [LINK: ([320]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:2-321]  Separately, Plamen Dimitrov, President of the Confederation of Bulgarian Trade Unions, also criticized Radev's push for a euro referendum calling it a misguided move that creates unnecessary controversy. Speaking on BNT, Dimitrov expressed his surprise and disappointment at the timing of the President's proposal, given that the issue has already been addressed by the Constitutional Court. [LINK: ([320]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:2-321]

On 12 May, Radev officially submitted his request for a referendum to the National Assembly, citing a lack of societal consensus on the question, as well as alleged government inaction in order to prepare for the adoption of the Euro as his official motives. [LINK: ([321]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-322]  The Chairwoman of the National Assembly,  [LINK: (Nataliya Kiselova) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nataliya_Kiselova] , however, decided to return the referendum-proposal, citing it as unconstitutional. [LINK: ([322]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-323]  Speaking after the return of the referendum, Radev characterised Kiselova's decision as flagrantly illegal and promised to challenge it in the Constitutional Court; he further argued that the referendum did not question the possible adoption of the Euro, but rather Bulgaria's readiness to adopt the Euro in 2026. [LINK: ([323]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-324]

On 26 May, Radev submitted his case to the Constitutional Court, and called on civil society to track potential rises in the price of goods, which he predicted would be a consequence of the introduction of the Euro. [LINK: ([324]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-325]  Speaking on 31 May, he stated that he believed in the need for a "political alternative" to oppose the "oligarchic model", and endorsed protests organised by Revival. [LINK: ([325]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-326]

Commenting the news of a positive convergence report by the European Commission and European Central Bank about Bulgaria's entrance into the Eurozone, Radev claimed that introducing the Euro would increase inflation, while admitting the potential positives including increased foreign investment. [LINK: ([326]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-327]

On 10 June, the Constitutional Court decided to dismiss all but one of Radev's submissions- that relating to whether Kiselova, as the speaker of the National Assembly, had the capacity to evaluate the constitutionality of a proposed-referendum. [LINK: ([327]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-328]

On 27 June, Radev claimed that prices had "increased significantly" after news of Bulgarian entrance into the Eurozone and accused the government of being unable to prevent inflation. [LINK: ([328]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-329]

After the official confirmation of Bulgaria's entrance into the Eurozone in 2026 on 8 July, Radev claimed that ascension into the Eurozone would be used as an excuse for GERB to remain in power for longer. [LINK: ([329]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:EuroBotash-330]

On 6 June, Radev approved the removal of head of SANS, Plamen Tonchev, who had been elected as Head of the Commission for the Review of Documents related to the Bulgarian State Security. [LINK: ([330]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-331]  The removal of Tonchev led to a long-term conflict between the Zhelyazkov government and President Radev, due to accusations by Zhelyazkov that Radev was obstructing key appointments within the security and diplomatic services. On 13 June, Boyko Borisov accused Radev of specifically obstructing the appointment of the Secretary to the Ministry of Interior, which Radev denied, claiming that no candidacy was submitted by the government. [LINK: ([331]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-332]  On 21 June, it was confirmed by Radev that the procedure for the selection of the next Secretary had commenced, involving both himself and representatives of the government. [LINK: ([332]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-333]  On 2 July, Minister of Interior,  [LINK: (Daniel Mitov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daniel_Mitov] , accused Radev of obstructing the appointment of the position. [LINK: ([333]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-334]

A sign of the distrust between the two institutions was indicated by Radev's non-inclusion in the Bulgarian delegation to the  [LINK: (2025 NATO summit) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_The_Hague_NATO_summit] , with it instead being led by Prime Minister Rosen Zhelyazkov. [LINK: ([334]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-335]

On 20 June, Boyko Borisov accused President Radev of harming the evacuation of Bulgarians from the Middle East due to his frequent state visits, which Radev called a lie, before accusing Borisov of corrupt practices during his time in office. [LINK: ([335]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-336]

After corruption scandals in Sofia, which led to the arrest of a number of Municipal Mayors related to  [LINK: (PP) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/We_Continue_the_Change] , he called for greater investigations into corruption, specifically related to figures in the current government. [LINK: ([336]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-337]  He condemned the arrest of Varna mayor,  [LINK: (Blagomir Kotsev) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blagomir_Kotsev] . [LINK: ([337]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-338]

In early July, an investigation was opened by the Prosecutors Office, as well as the Anti-Corruption Agency, into the agreement with the Turkish energy company, Botash, for the supply of natural gas to Bulgaria. [LINK: ([329]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:EuroBotash-330]  As the deal had been signed by the Radev appointed Donev caretaker government, Radev was criticised by politicians, specifically from GERB, for enabling the agreement. In a statement to the press, he defended the agreement with Botash, and claimed that any issues with it were caused by its execution. [LINK: ([329]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:EuroBotash-330]

On 24 July, the Constitutional Court ruled against the abolition of the new caretaker government appointment system created by the 2023 constitutional amendments. [LINK: ([338]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-339]  Radev argued that the ruling perpetuated the "disbalance in the separation of powers" and claimed the constitutional changes have put "loyalty" instead of competence as the primary criteria in the selection of caretaker governments. [LINK: ([339]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-340]

On 24 July, President Radev vetoed a new law about medical insurance, specifically due to his opposition to the imposition of a limit for public health coverage of health procedures. [LINK: ([340]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-341]

On 25 July, Prime Minister Rosen Zhelyazkov publicly accused Radev of obstructing the work of the government by refusing to appoint a new head of DANS, Secretary of the Ministry of Interior, as well as ambassadors to a number of countries. [LINK: ([341]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-342]  Radev, in turn, criticised the management of the wild-fires by the incumbent government, claiming that Bulgaria should not rely on foreign aid in reducing the fires. [LINK: ([342]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-343]  Specifically, Radev called for the urgent procurement of new fire-fighting helicopters as part of Bulgaria's air force. [LINK: ([343]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-344]

On 10 August, Radev accused the government of organising "the biggest theft since the 90s" after they announced their intention to sell 4,400 state properties which they stated were no longer necessary. [LINK: ([344]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-345]  Subsequently, he vetoed the proposed law about the sale of the properties, although his veto was overturned. [LINK: ([345]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-banker.bg-346]

Responding to accusations that he was sabotaging the current government, Radev claimed that he had on the contrary assisted the government, taking credit for attracting foreign investments from the German armament company,  [LINK: (Rheinmetall) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rheinmetall] . [LINK: ([346]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-347]  He also accused the government of obstructing his own appointments, including for the position of the National Security Service.

On 29 August, Radev approved the appointment of Miroslav Rashkov as the new Secretary of the Ministry of Interior. [LINK: ([345]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-banker.bg-346]   On 30 August, he confirmed that an agreement had been reached on the vacant ambassadorial positions, however accused the current government of organising a purge within the SANS. [LINK: ([347]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-348]

Speaking at the annual commemoration of the  [LINK: (Unification of Bulgaria) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_unification]  on 6 September, he asked attendees if they were "willing to hand Bulgaria over to the  [LINK: (derebey) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Derebey] ?", and called for national unity against those who "wish to sell our sovereignty, our budget and our identity". [LINK: ([348]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-349]

On 3 October, the parliament passed a law which reduced Presidential involvement in the appointment of key security services. The law was condemned by Radev, who claimed it was an excuse for a takeover of the security services and their use against the opposition. [LINK: ([349]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-350]  He vetoed the law on 14 October, motivating his decision by the potential politicization of the security services, although the vetoes was overturned by the National Assembly. [LINK: ([350]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-351]

After a law was passed which removed NSS-provided cars from members of the presidential administration, Radev announced that he would use his personal vehicle to travel in solidarity with his staff. [LINK: ([351]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-352]  In response, DPS leader, Delyan Peevski, proposed that cars and drivers previously used by the NSS are transferred to the presidency; a proposal which was endorsed by GERB. [LINK: ([352]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-353]  Radev rejected the proposal, claiming that the creation of a car park within the presidency would take too long. [LINK: ([353]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-354]

President Radev was highly critical of the 2026 budget proposed by the Zhelyazkov government and endorsed  [LINK: (protests against it) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_Bulgarian_budget_protests] . [LINK: ([354]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-355]  On 1 December, he officially called for the government's resignation and early elections. [LINK: ([355]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-356]  Asked about the possibility of forming a political party in upcoming legislative elections, Radev lamented that he would enter electoral politics "when it was least expected". [LINK: ([356]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-357]

Following  [LINK: (large-scale anti-government protests) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_Bulgarian_protests] , on 11 December the Zhelyazkov government resigned. [LINK: ([357]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-358]  Following Zhelyazkov's resignation, the President scheduled constitutionally-mandated consultations with all parliamentary groups.

During the consultations, all the parliamentary groups agreed that the formation of a new government within the current parliament was impossible and that new elections should be scheduled as soon as possible. [LINK: ([358]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:consult_Velich-359]  Furthermore, most of the parliamentary groups, PP-DB, Revival and MECh in particular, urged the President to schedule new elections in March or April, to avoid holding the election during the winter months. [LINK: ([359]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-360]  During the consultations, Radev further raised the question of amending the electoral code in order to ensure greater trust in the electoral process. [LINK: ([358]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:consult_Velich-359]  PP-DB, Revival, MECh and Velichie all expressed support for the re-introduction of voting through machine installations, while ITN and DPS-NN argued for the use of machine scanners to count votes.

During the consultations, Radev was asked by representatives from GERB, PP-DB and Revival about whether he planned to participate in the upcoming parliamentary elections with his own political party. [LINK: ([360]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-361]  Radev, however, refused to discuss the possibility of such a party during the consultations. After the conclusion of the consultations, Radev announced that he would present the first mandate for government formation in January 2026. [LINK: ([358]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-:consult_Velich-359]

On 31 December 2025, Radev delivered the traditional New Years address. During the address, he called for a societal mobilisation for upcoming elections in order to maintain the energy generated by the earlier protests, and criticised the introduction of the Euro without a referendum. [LINK: ([361]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-362]

On 12 January, he formally granted the first exploratory mandate to acting Prime Minister, Rosen Zhelyazkov, who had been nominated as GERB-SDS' Prime Ministerial candidate. Rosen Zhelyazkov returned the first mandate unfulfilled. [LINK: ([362]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-363]  On 14 January, the Prime Ministerial candidate nominated by PP-DB, Nadezhda Yordanova, similarly returned the second mandate unfulfilled immediately after it had been given to her by the President. [LINK: ([363]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-364]

Radev selected the Dogan-led  [LINK: (Alliance for Rights and Freedoms) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alliance_for_Rights_and_Freedoms]  to receive the third mandate, which was widely interpreted by analysts as a slight against DPS leader, Delyan Peevski. [LINK: ([364]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-365]  Despite speculation about the possibility of APS retaining the third mandate in order to explore government formation possibilities, the parliamentary group's representative,  [LINK: (Hayri Sadakov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hayri_Sadakov] , returned the third mandate unfulfilled on 16 January making snap legislative elections inevitable. [LINK: ([365]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-366]

With snap legislative elections confirmed, speculation about the possibility of a Radev-affiliated political project participating in them intensified. On 20 December 2025, a political party called "Third of March Movement" was founded and declared Radev as its "informal leader", Radev however publicly disavowed the party. [LINK: ([366]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-367]  On 14 January, when asked about the possibility of forming his own party, Radev claimed that his party already existed and represented the "anti-mafia consensus in Bulgaria". [LINK: ([367]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-368]

During an address to the nation on 19 January 2026, Radev announced his intention to resign. [LINK: ([368]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-369]  On 20 January, he submitted his resignation as President to the Constitutional Court. [LINK: ([369]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-370]  On 23 January, the Constitutional Court formally accepted Radev's resignation, thus ending his tenure as President of Bulgaria. [LINK: ([370]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-371]

Shortly after the ruling, Radev left the "Dondukov 2" palace together with acting President,  [LINK: (Iliana Iotova) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova] , and addressed supporters, promising a unified struggle at the upcoming legislative elections. [LINK: ([371]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-372]


# FOREIGN POLICY #

In February 2017, Radev condemned and called for an end to the  [LINK: (EU sanctions against Russia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_sanctions_during_the_Ukrainian_crisis] , while at the same time describing the  [LINK: (Annexation of Crimea by the Russian Federation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Annexation_of_Crimea_by_the_Russian_Federation]  as a "violation of international law". [LINK: ([372]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-373]  In April 2022, Radev broke ranks with the  [LINK: (Petkov Government) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petkov_Government] , declaring that he is fully opposed to Bulgaria providing weapons to Ukraine in relation to  [LINK: (Russia's invasion of the latter) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine] , characterizing it as a step towards the direct involvement of Bulgaria in the conflict and seeing it as contrary to the pursuit of a peaceful solution. [LINK: ([373]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-374]  On 23 September 2022, Radev came out with what has been described as "his strongest statement yet on the Russian-Ukrainian conflict", calling the  [LINK: (Russian organised referendums) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_annexation_referendums_in_Russian-occupied_Ukraine]  "unacceptable" and denouncing the use of "'the nuclear card'" by the Russian leadership. [LINK: ([374]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-375]

On 2 October 2022, Radev refused to sign a declaration produced by other Eastern and Central European Presidents, which endorsed  [LINK: (future Ukrainian ascension into NATO) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ukraine-NATO_relations] , citing the fact that discussions of Ukrainian membership were "too early" and had to involve collective decisions by NATO structures. [LINK: ([375]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-376]   Upon returning to Bulgaria from the NATO summit on 7 October, Radev reiterated his position that discussing Ukrainian ascension into NATO was too early, and further underlined his opposition to the provision of military aid to Ukraine. [LINK: ([376]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-377]

During the December 2022 EU summit to discuss the new sanctions package, Radev threatened to veto it, if it included sanctions on the export of nuclear fuel, and further called on fellow EU leaders to begin to "refocus" their work towards achieving a ceasefire in the Ukrainian conflict. [LINK: ([377]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-378]  In February 2023, Radev signed a joint-declaration with the presidents of 9 other Eastern and Central European countries which condemned Russian aggression against Ukraine. [LINK: ([378]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-379]  At the same time, Radev reiterated his opposition to the sending of lethal aid to Ukraine, including at a meeting with Hungarian prime minister,  [LINK: (Viktor Orban) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viktor_Orban]  on 2 February. [LINK: ([379]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-380]  During the March 2023 EU summit to discuss the procurement of 1 million shells for Ukraine within a year, Radev insisted that Bulgaria would not participate in it, and further claimed he would ensure that Bulgarian ammunition sold to third parties was not sent to Ukraine. [LINK: ([380]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-381]  Despite this opposition, Radev ended up supporting the European Council resolution which  included the provision of ammunition to Ukraine. [LINK: ([381]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-382]

In July 2023, President Radev met with Ukrainian president  [LINK: (Volodymyr Zelensky) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volodymyr_Zelensky] , as part of his visit to Bulgaria on 6 July. During the meeting, President Radev expressed sympathy for Ukraine, particularly due to the high human cost, however Radev also claimed that the war has "no military solution" and called for Ukraine to open negotiations with Russia in order to "deescalate". [LINK: ([382]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-383]  From his end, Zelensky criticised Radev's use of the word "conflict", instead of "war", as it gave credence to the idea of two equal parties – pointing out Russia's status as an aggressor-state and the fact that only Russia could be responsible for de-escalation. Zelensky further criticised Radev for his stance on the provision of military aid, noting that Ukraine and the nations of the European Union had a shared interest in restraining Russia in Ukraine. In the aftermath of the meeting, Radev was criticised by the Denkov government, as well as Ukrainian and international media, for reiterating Russian talking points. [LINK: ([383]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-384]  [LINK: ([384]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-385]  Radev, in turn, criticised the government for "not consulting him" before inviting President Zelensky, as well as not putting the national interest of Bulgaria first. [LINK: ([385]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-386]

On 14 July, President Radev claimed that "Ukraine insists on the continuation of this war, but lets be clear, the bill is paid by the whole of Europe", further calling on the Bulgarian government to adopt an independent foreign policy and cease increased lethal aid for Ukraine. [LINK: ([386]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-387]  During his speech to the UN Annual Global Summit, Radev condemned "the Russian aggression against Ukraine", and further called for the 78th annual session of the UN to work towards "finding peaceful avenues to the resolution of the crisis". [LINK: ([387]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-388]  In September 2023, Radev was accused by some media outlets of avoiding a meeting with Ukrainian president Zelensky during the annual UN conference in New York. [LINK: ([388]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-389]

Radev has opposed the dispatch of NATO member soldiers into Ukraine, even in a unilateral capacity, believing that this may trigger a war between the Russian Federation and NATO. [LINK: ([389]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-390]  Radev has generally been supportive of the  [LINK: (Trump administration's) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreign_policy_of_the_second_Donald_Trump_administration]  approach towards Russia, expressing hope that Trump's policy will lead to an end of hostilities in Ukraine and has urged the European Union to adopt a similar foreign policy towards Russia. [LINK: ([390]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-391]  [LINK: ([391]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-392]  [LINK: ([392]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-393]

Radev was critical of the "re-armament fund" proposed by the European commission, claiming that it would substantially increase European debt. [LINK: ([393]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-394]

On 17 March 2017, Radev condemned as 'absolutely unacceptable' what he described as a Turkish intervention in Bulgaria's  [LINK: (2017 parliamentary election) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2017_Bulgarian_parliamentary_election]  after the Turkish ambassador to Bulgaria was found to have appeared in a campaign clip for one of Bulgaria's political parties and after Turkish Social Affairs minister was found to have agitated and offer incentives for  [LINK: (Bulgarian Turks in Turkey) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_Turks_in_Turkey]  to cross the border in an organized voting campaign and vote for the same party. Radev stated that he had referred the matter to the  [LINK: (European Union) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Union] . [LINK: ([394]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-395]  He met with Turkish president  [LINK: (Recep Tayyip Erdoğan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recep_Tayyip_Erdo%C4%9Fan]  several months later in July, following which he described Turkey as an "important neighbour, partner and ally", while at the same time stating that this status hinged on Turkey's respect for Bulgaria's  "internal political process, regarding Bulgaria's political parties and electoral system". He also became the only EU  [LINK: (head of state) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Head_of_state]  to attend Erdoğan's inauguration, stating that his mandate was not given to him by either the  [LINK: (European Commission) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Commission]  or the Bulgarian Government, but by the Bulgarian people. [LINK: ([395]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-396]

On 24 January 2018, Radev condemned the  [LINK: (Turkish invasion of northern Syria) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Olive_Branch]  aimed at ousting U.S.-backed  [LINK: (Syrian Kurds) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People%27s_Protection_Units]  from the enclave of  [LINK: (Afrin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Afrin,_Syria] , and insisted that the European Union should intervene to stop it. [LINK: ([396]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-397]  [LINK: ([397]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-398]  During the campaign for the Bulgarian legislative elections in 2021, Radev accused Erdoğan of meddling with Bulgaria's internal affairs after participating in a video-conference with Bulgarian political party,  [LINK: (DPS) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Movement_for_Rights_and_Freedoms] . [LINK: ([398]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-399]  In December 2022, Radev visited Turkish president Erdoğan, achieving a deal to enhance border security and increase cooperation in the energy sector. [LINK: ([399]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-400]

On 29 May 2023, Radev congratulated Erdoğan with his re-election after the  [LINK: (2023 Turkish Presidential Election) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2023_Turkish_general_election] , [LINK: ([400]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-401]  and later attended his inauguration, being the only European Union head of state to do so. [LINK: ([401]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-402]  During the inauguration, Radev and Erdoğan had a short meeting, during which Radev underlined the progress being made in economic relations between the two countries, particularly focusing on the recently signed agreement with Turkish gas company, Botas. [LINK: ([402]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-403]

Rumen Radev has been critical of the  [LINK: (EU accession) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enlargement_of_the_European_Union]  of the  [LINK: (Republic of North Macedonia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republic_of_North_Macedonia] . [LINK: ([403]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-404]  Radev has opposed the possibility of lifting the veto on North Macedonia's Eurointegration, at the very least until Bulgarians are added to the  [LINK: (Constitution of North Macedonia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constitution_of_North_Macedonia] . [LINK: ([404]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-405]  Radev has also asked for increased  [LINK: (European Union) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Union] -level action to prevent hate speech against Bulgarians living in North Macedonia. [LINK: ([405]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-406]

Radev condemned the behaviour of the newly elected president of North Macedonia,  [LINK: (Gordana Siljanovska-Davkova) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gordana_Siljanovska-Davkova] , and called for North Macedonia to adhere to existing treaties. The president maintained that the reluctance to observe the Treaty of Friendship with Bulgaria, signed in 2017, is an obstacle to the development of bilateral relations. [LINK: ([406]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-407]

In early September 2024, Radev held an unofficial meeting with the new North Macedonian President, at which key bilateral issues between the two countries were discussed. [LINK: ([407]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-408]  The unofficial meeting, which according to both sides had taken place in a pleasant and constructive atmosphere, provoked severely negative reactions from North Macedonian politicians, after a photo of the two President's was published in which only the Bulgarian flag was present. [LINK: ([408]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-409]

In June 2025, Radev sent a letter to European Union leaders denouncing the report about Macedonia's EU accession, which included the recognition of the Macedonian language and identity, and was categorised as being "weak" on the rights of Bulgarians by Bulgarian politicians. [LINK: ([409]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-410]

On the day of the attack by Hamas on Israel, Radev made a statement on X condemning the Hamas-led attack on Israel and expressing solidarity with Israel. [LINK: ([410]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-411]  On 11 October, Radev held a national security meeting with the cabinet and representatives of the security apparatus, which excluded members of parliament, as in a traditional Consulative Council for National Security. [LINK: ([411]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-412]  Following this meeting, Radev and the prime minister, Denkov, came out with a joint-press briefing, at which Radev declared there was no imminent national security risk due to the Hamas-led attack, although the risk of migrant pressure on the border with Turkey, as well as possible infiltration by terrorist groups had increased. [LINK: ([412]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-413]

Radev participated in a "prayer for peace" within the Central Sofia Synagogue, together with many other Bulgarian politicians, expressing disgust at "the cruel and violent acts committed by the terrorists" and solidarity with Israel. [LINK: ([413]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-414]  During his speech at the  [LINK: (Seventy-ninth session of the United Nations General Assembly) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seventy-ninth_session_of_the_United_Nations_General_Assembly] , Radev called for an immediate ceasefire in  [LINK: (Gaza) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaza_war]  and called on  [LINK: (Hamas) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamas]  to release all the Israeli  [LINK: (hostages) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaza_war_hostage_crisis] . [LINK: ([414]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-415]

In April 2018, he criticised the  [LINK: (2018 missile strikes against Syria) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2018_missile_strikes_against_Syria] , instead calling for "less weapons and more dialogue". [LINK: ([415]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-416]  [LINK: ([416]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-417]  In February 2019, Radev condemned the  [LINK: (Bulgarian Government) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Third_Borisov_Government] 's recognition of Venezuelan opposition leader  [LINK: (Juan Guaidó) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Guaid%C3%B3]  as interim  [LINK: (president of Venezuela) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Venezuela]  during the  [LINK: (2019 Venezuelan presidential crisis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2019_Venezuelan_presidential_crisis] , adding that he believed the government had overstepped its authority in recognizing the opposition leader as interim president. Radev further criticised the EU's recognition of Guaido, urging both the country and the EU to remain neutral and refrain from recognizing Guaido, as he viewed such recognition as imposing an  [LINK: (ultimatum) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ultimatum] , which he deemed would only aggravate the crisis in Venezuela. [LINK: ([417]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-418]  [LINK: ([418]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-419]

Radev has been very critical of the positions of Austria and the Netherlands, who in December 2022 announced they would veto the ascension of Bulgaria into the  [LINK: (Schengen Area) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schengen_Area] , calling the decision "cynical" and in contrast with the concept of European solidarity. [LINK: ([419]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-420]  During an EU summit on 15 December 2022, Radev called on EU leaders to give a clear time table for Bulgaria's membership in the Schengen Area before the end of 2023, stating that Schengen membership was critical to "Bulgarian dignity as part of the European family". [LINK: ([420]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-421]  Radev further continued to insist that there were no valid reasons for Bulgaria's non-ascension into the Schengen Area, citing a 2011 European Commission Report which found Bulgaria ready to join the Schengen Area. [LINK: ([421]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-422]


# APPROVAL RATINGS #

Radev enjoyed positive approval ratings for the vast majority of his presidency. Having been elected with around 60% of the vote in the  [LINK: (autumn 2016 election) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2016_Bulgarian_presidential_election] , he managed to keep that figure as his approval rating through to 2018. [LINK: ([422]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-423]

His approval then rose to 67% by May 2018, [LINK: ([423]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-424]  before falling to around 56% by autumn 2019. It is noteworthy, however, that even after this fall in his popularity, he was still considered the most popular and approved of Bulgarian politician, as well as one of the only two Bulgarian politicians (the other being  [LINK: (Maya Manolova) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maya_Manolova] ) with a higher percentage of approval than disapproval. [LINK: ([424]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-425]  [LINK: ([425]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-426]  [LINK: ([426]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-427]  By April 2020, Radev's approval ratings stood at about 49%. [LINK: ([427]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-428]  Radev began his second term as president with an approval rating of 58.5%, according to a  [LINK: (Gallup poll) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gallup_(company)] . [LINK: ([428]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-429]  Radev has declined in popularity in opinion polls, with Radev's approval rating reaching its lowest point of 33% in an Alpha Research poll in June 2023. [LINK: ([429]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-430]

Radev's approval rating recovered throughout 2024 and 2025, averaging around 40% before his resignation in January 2026. [LINK: ([430]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-431]


# FAMILY AND PERSONAL LIFE #

Radev joined the  [LINK: (Bulgarian Communist Party) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_Communist_Party]  in the 1980s. He later stated that his primary reason for joining the party had been so that he would be deployed to fly in a  [LINK: (supersonic jet) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supersonic_aircraft] , but also added that he was not ashamed of his past and was proud of the things he did. He left the party in 1990, when a newly enacted law forbade members of the country's armed forces from being members of political parties. [LINK: ([431]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-432]  [LINK: ([432]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-433]  He has not been a member of any political parties since and his candidacy in the 2016 elections was supported by an independent initiative committee affiliated with the  [LINK: (Bulgarian Socialist Party) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_Socialist_Party] , rather than by a formal nomination by any party. [LINK: ([433]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-434]

Radev has two children from his first marriage to Ginka Radeva, which ended in a divorce in 2014: a daughter Darina, born in 2001 and a son Georgi, born in 2003. He later married  [LINK: (Desislava Gencheva) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Desislava_Radeva] , who was previously married to the BSP MP  [LINK: (Georgi Svilenski) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georgi_Svilenski] . Apart from  [LINK: (Bulgarian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_language] , Radev is also fluent in  [LINK: (Russian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_language] ,  [LINK: (German) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_language]  and  [LINK: (English) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/English_language] . [LINK: ([434]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-435]  Radev's father died on 3 April 2020. [LINK: ([435]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-436]

Radev is a member of the  [LINK: (Bulgarian Orthodox Church) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_Orthodox_Church] . [LINK: ([436]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-437]  He stated his support for the efforts of the Bulgarian church to introduce religious education in Bulgarian schools and declared that "the support of the Bulgarian Orthodox Church and the spiritual well-being of the faithful remains the first priority for the State". [LINK: ([437]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-438]  [LINK: ([438]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-439]  Radev also expressed his admiration for  [LINK: (Pope Francis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Francis] , calling him "the voice of the weak and the underprivileged". [LINK: ([439]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-440]


# MILITARY CAREER #

1987 – 1989: Junior pilot in the 15th Fighter Aviation Regiment –  [LINK: (Ravnets) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ravnets_Air_Base]

1989 – 1992: Unit deputy commander at the 15th Fighter Aviation Regiment – Ravnets

1992 – 1997: Unit commander at the 15th Fighter Aviation Regiment – Ravnets

1997 – 1999: MiG-29 squadron commander at the Fifth Fighter Airbase – Ravnets

1999 – 2000: Deputy commander for flight preparation at the Fifth Fighter Airbase – Ravnets

2000: Deputy commander for flight training at the Third Fighter Airbase –  [LINK: (Graf Ignatievo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graf_Ignatievo_Air_Base]

2000 – Study of the Air defence of the Republic of Bulgaria – NATO, Brussels

2000 – 2002: Chief of Staff of the Third Fighter Airbase – Graf Ignatievo

2002 – 2004: Chief of Staff of the Third Fighter Airbase – Graf Ignatievo

2004 – 2009: Commander of the Third Fighter Airbase – Graf Ignatievo

2009 – 2014: Bulgarian Air Force deputy commander

2014 – 2017: Bulgarian Air Force commanderRadev in 2012

Pilot 1st class.

Flight experience of  [LINK: (L-29) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L-29] ,  [LINK: (L-39) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aero_L-39_Albatros]  trainers and  [LINK: (MiG-15UTI) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MiG-15] ,  [LINK: (MiG-17) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MiG-17] ,  [LINK: (MiG-21) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MiG-21] ,  [LINK: (MiG-29) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MiG-29]  fighter jets.

Familiarization flights of the  [LINK: (F-15) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-15] ,  [LINK: (F-16) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-16] ,  [LINK: (F/A-18 Hornet) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F/A-18_Hornet] ,  [LINK: (Eurofighter Typhoon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eurofighter_Typhoon] , SAAB  [LINK: (Gripen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gripen] ,  [LINK: (Dassault Rafale) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dassault_Rafale] .

Flown over 1400 hours.

In 2014, he organized aviation show "This We Are!" and personally performed the " [LINK: (Bell) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tailslide] " and " [LINK: (Pugachev's Cobra) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pugachev%27s_Cobra] " manoeuvres on a MiG-29. [LINK: ([440]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-441]  [LINK: ([441]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-442]

1987: Lieutenant

1989: Senior lieutenant

1994: Captain

1997: Major

1999: Lieutenant colonel

2002: Colonel

2007: Brigadier general

2014: Major general

2017: General (Commander-in-Chief)

Rumen Radev was awarded numerous medals and prizes, including the sign "For loyal service under the flags" – III degree, and Honorary sign of the Ministry of Defence "Saint George" – II degree. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev#cite_note-CV-2]


